/* English B - topic drills, round 3: original questions modelled on IB Paper 1 (writing), Paper 2 (reading and listening) and individual oral tasks. All texts are original; names and places marked fictional are invented. */
IB.addDrill("engb", {
  "engb-1": {
    units: { a: "Lifestyles & health", b: "Language & identity", c: "Beliefs & subcultures" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+2", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Why we swim in the sea in January</b><p><b>[1]</b> At 7 a.m. on a grey January Saturday, around thirty people in woolly hats gather on the pebbles at Harlow Bay. Some are students, some are grandparents, and one is a surgeon who has just finished a night shift. Within minutes they are up to their shoulders in water that is barely eight degrees.</p><p><b>[2]</b> Cold-water swimming has grown rapidly in popularity over the last decade. Enthusiasts claim it lifts their mood, strengthens their immune system and helps them sleep. Doctors are more cautious. 'The first shock of cold water raises the heart rate sharply,' says sports physician Dr Ines Okafor. 'For a healthy person that is usually harmless, but anyone with a heart condition should seek advice first.'</p><p><b>[3]</b> For many swimmers, the real attraction is not physical at all. 'Nobody here cares what job you do or what you look like in a swimsuit,' explains Petra, who founded the group after her divorce. 'We are all equally ridiculous, shivering together.' The group now runs a hot-drinks stall and a monthly beach clean.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Newcomers are advised never to swim alone, to enter the water slowly and to stay in for only a few minutes at first. As Petra puts it, 'The sea will still be here next week. Be patient with it.'</p><p><i>All names in this text are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Find the word in paragraph 1 that means 'small smooth stones on a beach'.</p><p>(b) Find the word in paragraph 2 that means 'people who are very keen on an activity'.</p><p>(c) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Doctors fully share the swimmers' enthusiasm.'</p><p>(d) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The group was started by someone going through a difficult time.'</p><p>(e) In 'Be patient with it' (paragraph 4), what does 'it' refer to?</p><p>(f) Identify two pieces of advice given to newcomers in paragraph 4.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) pebbles [1]", "(b) Enthusiasts [1]", "(c) False - 'Doctors are more cautious' [1]", "(d) True - 'who founded the group after her divorce' [1]", "(e) the sea [1]", "(f) never swim alone [1]", "(f) enter the water slowly [1]", "(f) stay in for only a few minutes at first [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>For many swimmers, the real attraction is not physical at all. 'Nobody here cares what job you do or what you look like in a swimsuit,' explains Petra. 'We are all equally ridiculous, shivering together.'</p></blockquote><p>According to the text, what attracts many swimmers to the group?</p>", options: ["The health benefits of cold water", "A sense of equality and belonging", "The chance to meet medical professionals", "The competitive races held each week"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (A sense of equality and belonging) - 'Nobody here cares what job you do' and 'equally ridiculous, shivering together' show that the attraction is social equality and belonging."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>From next March, shops in the Republic of Valdora will no longer be allowed to sell high-caffeine energy drinks to anyone under sixteen. The health ministry says a single can may contain as much caffeine as two cups of coffee, and teachers have reported pupils arriving at school 'wired' and unable to concentrate. Retailers, however, warn that the ban will simply push teenagers towards online sellers, where age checks are easier to avoid.</p><p><i>Valdora is a fictional country.</i></p></blockquote><p>Complete the following sentences with words taken from the text.</p><p>(a) The ban on selling energy drinks to under-16s will begin ...</p><p>(b) The ministry says one can may contain the same amount of caffeine as ...</p><p>(c) Retailers fear that teenagers will buy the drinks from ...</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) next March [1]", "(b) two cups of coffee [1]", "(c) online sellers [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Sleep-tracking apps promise to tell you exactly how well you slept. Ironically, some users become so anxious about their nightly scores that they lie awake worrying, a condition researchers have nicknamed 'orthosomnia'. Experts suggest treating the numbers as a rough guide rather than a verdict.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Which word in the text signals that the result is the opposite of what was intended?</p><p>(b) According to the text, what is 'orthosomnia'?</p><p>(c) Find the word in the text that means 'a final judgement'.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) Ironically [1]", "(b) worrying about sleep-tracker scores so much that you lie awake at night [1]", "(c) verdict [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your town has opened a free outdoor gym in the park, but very few teenagers use it. Write a text to encourage young people to try it and to make exercise part of their routine. Choose one text type: social media post, leaflet, or speech at a school assembly. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs specific, motivating reasons (health, mood, friendship, cost) and practical details (location, times, beginner tips); high C needs the conventions of the chosen type and a lively register suited to teenagers.", pts: ["Message: benefits of regular exercise for body and mind, with concrete examples (sleep, stress, energy).", "Message: practical information about the gym - free, location, opening times, simple routines for beginners.", "Message: tackles barriers such as embarrassment or lack of time, and ends with a clear call to action.", "Social media post conventions: catchy opening, hashtags, short paragraphs, emojis used sparingly, tagging friends.", "Leaflet conventions: title and slogan, headings, bullet points, contact or location details.", "Speech conventions: greeting the audience, rhetorical questions, direct address 'you', closing thanks.", "Language: imperatives and persuasive structures used accurately, varied vocabulary (stamina, routine, well-being)."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>The accent I tried to hide</b><p><b>[1]</b> For most of my first year at university I practised sounding like somebody else. I had grown up in a small fishing town in the north, where we drop our 'h's and call everyone 'love', and within a week of arriving in the capital a classmate laughed and asked whether I had 'just got off the boat'.</p><p><b>[2]</b> So I copied the newsreaders. I flattened my vowels, slowed my speech and stopped saying 'love'. It worked, in a way: nobody commented on my voice any more. But when I phoned home, my mother said I sounded 'like a stranger reading the weather'.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Linguists will tell you that there is no such thing as a 'correct' accent, only accents that carry more prestige in certain places. Yet studies suggest that listeners make quick judgements about intelligence and trustworthiness based on how people sound - judgements that say more about the listener than the speaker.</p><p><b>[4]</b> These days I let my accent come and go. In job interviews I still smooth it out a little; with friends it returns at full strength. I no longer see this as hiding. It is simply knowing that I own more than one voice.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Which phrase in paragraph 1 shows that the writer's accent was mocked?</p><p>(b) Find the word in paragraph 2 that means 'made more even or less varied'.</p><p>(c) What did the writer's mother mean when she said the writer sounded 'like a stranger reading the weather'?</p><p>(d) Find the word in paragraph 3 that means 'status or respect'.</p><p>(e) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'According to paragraph 3, listeners' judgements about accents are reliable.'</p><p>(f) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The writer now speaks in the same way in every situation.'</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) 'a classmate laughed and asked whether I had just got off the boat' [1]", "(b) flattened [1]", "(c) the writer sounded unfamiliar / too formal / unnatural / like a different person [1]", "(d) prestige [1]", "(e) False - 'judgements that say more about the listener than the speaker' [1]", "(f) False - 'In job interviews I still smooth it out a little; with friends it returns at full strength' [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>These days I let my accent come and go. In job interviews I still smooth it out a little; with friends it returns at full strength. I no longer see this as hiding. It is simply knowing that I own more than one voice.</p></blockquote><p>Which statement best describes the writer's attitude in this paragraph?</p>", options: ["The writer is ashamed of their original accent.", "The writer has given up their original accent completely.", "The writer accepts adapting their accent to the situation as part of who they are.", "The writer believes interviewers should ignore accents."], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (The writer accepts adapting their accent to the situation as part of who they are.) - 'I no longer see this as hiding' and 'I own more than one voice' show acceptance of adapting to context."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Four language learners</b><p><b>A. Kenji:</b> I learned Spanish from TV dramas before I ever opened a textbook. My grammar was terrible, but my slang was perfect.</p><p><b>B. Amira:</b> At home we speak Arabic, but my children answer me in English. I worry that the language will disappear with my generation.</p><p><b>C. Tomasz:</b> Moving abroad at forty, I felt like a child again. I couldn't even buy a train ticket without asking someone for help.</p><p><b>D. Leila:</b> I switch to French when I want to sound serious and to Persian when I tell jokes. Each language gives me a different personality.</p></blockquote><p>Which person (A, B, C or D)... Write the letter and the name.</p><p>(a) fears that a language will not be passed on to the next generation?</p><p>(b) felt dependent on other people?</p><p>(c) learned a language informally through entertainment?</p><p>(d) feels that each language expresses a different side of them?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) B / Amira [1]", "(b) C / Tomasz [1]", "(c) A / Kenji [1]", "(d) D / Leila [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>When the last fluent speaker of a language dies, linguists describe the language as 'extinct', even if recordings and dictionaries of it survive. Ethnologue, a widely used catalogue of the world's languages, lists more than 7,000 living languages and classifies around 40% of them as endangered. Many have fewer than a thousand speakers, most of them elderly.</p></blockquote><p>(a) According to the text, when is a language described as extinct?</p><p>(b) Complete the sentence with words from the text: About 40% of living languages are classified as ...</p><p>(c) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'A language is not considered extinct if dictionaries of it still exist.'</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) when its last fluent speaker dies [1]", "(b) endangered [1]", "(c) False - 'even if recordings and dictionaries of it survive' [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school is organising a 'Language Heritage Day' on which students share the languages spoken in their homes. Write a text explaining why home languages matter and encouraging students to take part. Choose one text type: speech at a school assembly, article for the school magazine, or email to parents. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs developed reasons (identity, family links, cognitive and career benefits) and concrete ideas for the day; high C needs register matched to students or parents and the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: link between language and identity, family history and belonging, with a personal example.", "Message: benefits of bilingualism (communication with relatives, cultural understanding, future careers).", "Message: concrete activities for the day (greetings wall, food stalls, songs, poetry readings) and how to take part.", "Speech conventions: greeting the audience, signposting, rhetorical questions, closing thanks.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, engaging lead paragraph, subheadings.", "Email conventions: subject line, polite greeting to parents, clear purpose, sign-off with name and role.", "Language: accurate complex sentences and topic vocabulary (heritage, bilingual, mother tongue, belonging)."] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", t: "General conversation (theme: Identities). After the extract discussion, the teacher asks: 'Do you think the languages we speak shape who we are?' Outline how you would develop and sustain your answer.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B2 5-6 needs a clear, developed opinion with relevant examples; C 5-6 needs spontaneous, sustained interaction (extending answers, responding to follow-up questions).", pts: ["Clear opinion with reasons: language carries culture, humour, values and ways of seeing the world.", "Examples: words with no direct translation, code-switching with family and friends, feeling different in another language.", "Personal or cultural connection: languages spoken at home, in school or in the candidate's community.", "Nuance: identity is also shaped by experiences and relationships, not language alone.", "Interaction: inviting the teacher's view, asking for clarification, building on follow-up questions."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+2+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>More than a costume</b><p><b>[1]</b> Every spring, the exhibition centre on the edge of the city fills with dragons, space pilots and cartoon heroes. The NorthCon convention attracted 18,000 visitors last year, most of them aged between fifteen and thirty, and many had spent months building their outfits by hand.</p><p><b>[2]</b> To outsiders, cosplay - dressing up as characters from films, comics and games - can look like an expensive form of escapism. Fans see it differently. 'People assume we are hiding from real life,' says Jun, 22, who studies engineering. 'Actually, I learned to use a 3D printer, sew and solder because of cosplay. It made me braver, not more withdrawn.'</p><p><b>[3]</b> The community has its own unwritten rules. The most important, repeated on signs throughout the hall, is 'Cosplay is not consent': visitors must ask before photographing or touching anyone in costume. Newcomers are also expected to praise effort rather than mock cheap materials.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Not everything is harmonious. Some long-time fans complain that social media has turned a playful hobby into a competition for likes, with professional cosplayers earning sponsorship deals. 'It used to be about the characters,' sighs one veteran. 'Now it is about the camera.'</p><p><i>NorthCon and the people quoted are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) How many visitors attended NorthCon last year?</p><p>(b) Find the word in paragraph 2 that means 'avoiding reality by doing something enjoyable'.</p><p>(c) Identify two skills Jun has learned through cosplay.</p><p>(d) What does the rule 'Cosplay is not consent' require visitors to do?</p><p>(e) In 'It used to be about the characters' (paragraph 4), what does 'It' refer to?</p><p>(f) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'All members of the community agree that social media has improved cosplay.'</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) 18,000 [1]", "(b) escapism [1]", "(c) using a 3D printer [1]", "(c) sewing [1]", "(c) soldering [1]", "(d) ask before photographing or touching anyone in costume / ask permission [1]", "(e) cosplay / the hobby [1]", "(f) False - 'Some long-time fans complain that social media has turned a playful hobby into a competition for likes' [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The community has its own unwritten rules. The most important is 'Cosplay is not consent'. Newcomers are also expected to praise effort rather than mock cheap materials.</p></blockquote><p>According to the text, newcomers are expected to:</p>", options: ["buy professionally made costumes", "praise effort instead of mocking cheap materials", "avoid taking any photographs at all", "enter competitions for prizes"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (praise effort instead of mocking cheap materials) - The text says newcomers are 'expected to praise effort rather than mock cheap materials'."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Every summer, thousands of walkers, religious and non-religious alike, follow the ancient pilgrim routes to Santiago de Compostela in north-west Spain. For some the journey is an act of faith; for others it is a chance to switch off from work. Walkers collect stamps in a 'pilgrim passport' at hostels along the way, and those who complete at least the final 100 km on foot can receive a certificate, the Compostela.</p></blockquote><p>(a) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Only religious people walk the route.'</p><p>(b) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Walkers must cover the whole route on foot to receive a certificate.'</p><p>(c) What do walkers collect at hostels along the way?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) False - 'religious and non-religious alike' [1]", "(b) False - 'those who complete at least the final 100 km on foot can receive a certificate' [1]", "(c) stamps (in a pilgrim passport) [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "A national newspaper has published an article describing young people who follow a plant-based diet for ethical reasons as 'simply following a fashion'. Write a text responding to the article and explaining the beliefs behind such choices. Choose one text type: letter to the editor, opinion column, or blog post. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear response to the article's claim, developed with ethical, environmental and personal reasons and an acknowledgement of other views; high C needs the conventions and register of the chosen type.", pts: ["Message: refers directly to the article and its claim, then challenges it with reasoned arguments.", "Message: beliefs behind the choice - animal welfare, environmental impact, health, religious or cultural values.", "Message: concedes that some people follow trends, but argues that this does not make the beliefs less genuine.", "Letter conventions: 'Dear Editor', reference to the article's title and date, formal tone, name and town at the end.", "Opinion column conventions: headline, byline, provocative opening, rhetorical questions, strong conclusion.", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, direct address to readers, invitation to comment.", "Language: discursive connectors (admittedly, nevertheless, furthermore) and precise vocabulary (ethical, sustainable, conviction)."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Skateboarding was once banned from many city squares and dismissed as a nuisance. Then, at the Tokyo 2020 Olympics, held in 2021, it appeared at the Games for the first time, and teenagers won several of the medals. Some older skaters were delighted; others feared that judges, rules and sponsors would destroy the rebellious spirit that had made the sport special.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Find the word in the text that means 'something annoying or troublesome'.</p><p>(b) Which detail shows that young people were successful in Olympic skateboarding?</p><p>(c) What did some older skaters fear?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) nuisance [1]", "(b) teenagers won several of the medals [1]", "(c) judges, rules and sponsors would destroy the rebellious spirit of the sport [1]"] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-2": {
    units: { a: "Travel & leisure", b: "Migration", c: "Life stories & rites of passage" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+2", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Can a two-week trip change the world?</b><p><b>[1]</b> Each year, many thousands of young people from wealthy countries pay to spend a few weeks building classrooms or teaching English abroad. The industry, known as 'voluntourism', is promoted with images of smiling children and the promise of a life-changing experience.</p><p><b>[2]</b> Critics argue that the life being changed is usually the volunteer's. Unskilled visitors may lay bricks that local builders, who need the work, could lay better and more cheaply. In some reported cases, walls built by one group of volunteers were quietly taken down so that the next group could build them again.</p><p><b>[3]</b> The concern is greatest in orphanages. Child-protection experts warn that a constant stream of short-term carers can harm children who have already experienced loss, because the children form attachments that are repeatedly broken. Several governments have now discouraged or banned orphanage volunteering.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Supporters reply that well-run programmes do exist. The best, they say, match volunteers' real skills - a qualified nurse, an engineer - to needs identified by local communities, and last months rather than weeks. 'Ask who asked for you,' advises one organiser. 'If the answer is nobody, stay home and donate instead.'</p></blockquote><p>(a) Find the word in paragraph 1 that means 'advertised'.</p><p>(b) According to paragraph 2, whose life is usually changed by voluntourism?</p><p>(c) Give one reason why building work by unskilled volunteers may be a problem.</p><p>(d) Why can short-term volunteering harm children in orphanages?</p><p>(e) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Some governments have taken action against orphanage volunteering.'</p><p>(f) Identify two features of well-run programmes, according to paragraph 4.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) promoted [1]", "(b) the volunteer's [1]", "(c) local builders need the work [1]", "(c) local builders could do it better or more cheaply [1]", "(d) the children form attachments that are repeatedly broken [1]", "(e) True - 'Several governments have now discouraged or banned orphanage volunteering' [1]", "(f) they match volunteers' real skills to local needs [1]", "(f) the needs are identified by local communities [1]", "(f) they last months rather than weeks [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>After two years of cancelled flights, many families rediscovered the 'staycation': a holiday spent in their own country, or even their own town. Tourism boards were quick to seize the opportunity, promoting lakes and hill-walking routes that locals had long taken for granted.</p></blockquote><p>The phrase 'taken for granted' suggests that local people had:</p>", options: ["never heard of the lakes and routes", "not fully appreciated the lakes and routes", "been banned from visiting the lakes and routes", "grown tired of travelling abroad"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (not fully appreciated the lakes and routes) - To take something for granted is to fail to value it because it is always there."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Parkrun began in 2004 with thirteen runners in a London park. Today the free, timed 5 km events take place every Saturday morning in parks around the world. Organisers insist it is 'a run, not a race': walkers are welcome, and every event has a volunteer 'tail walker' who finishes last so that nobody else has to.</p></blockquote><p>(a) How much does it cost to take part in Parkrun?</p><p>(b) Which phrase shows that people who do not run are welcome to take part?</p><p>(c) In 'so that nobody else has to', what does nobody else have to do?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) nothing / it is free [1]", "(b) 'walkers are welcome' [1]", "(c) finish last [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Dark-sky reserves, areas where artificial lighting is strictly controlled, have become an unexpected tourist attraction. In places such as the Aoraki Mackenzie reserve in New Zealand, guides lead groups on night walks and lend telescopes to visitors who have never seen the Milky Way. Local businesses welcome the visitors, who often come in winter when other tourists stay away, but some residents grumble about headlights on quiet roads after midnight.</p></blockquote><p>Which <b>two</b> of the following statements are true according to the text?</p><p>A. Lighting is strictly controlled in dark-sky reserves.<br>B. Visitors must bring their own telescopes.<br>C. Dark-sky tourists help local businesses in the quieter season.<br>D. All residents welcome the night-time visitors.<br>E. Dark-sky reserves exist only in New Zealand.</p>", options: ["A and C", "A and D", "B and C", "C and E"], answer: 0, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: A (A and C) - A: 'artificial lighting is strictly controlled'; C: visitors 'come in winter when other tourists stay away' and businesses welcome them. B is wrong (guides lend telescopes), D is wrong (some residents grumble), E is wrong ('such as' shows there are others)."] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your class recently went on a five-day school trip on which mobile phones were not allowed. Write a text reflecting on the experience and what you learned from it. Choose one text type: blog post, diary entry, or article for the school magazine. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs reflection, not just a list of activities: how feelings changed over the five days and what the writer takes away; high C needs the chosen type's conventions (a diary is private and intimate, a blog and article address readers).", pts: ["Message: initial reaction (anxiety, boredom, fear of missing out) contrasted with later feelings.", "Message: specific moments - conversations, noticing the landscape, games, getting lost without a map app.", "Message: conclusion about habits, attention and friendships, with a realistic resolution.", "Diary conventions: date, intimate first person, emotional and reflective tone, no audience address.", "Blog conventions: catchy title, date, direct address to readers, invitation to comment.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph, subheadings, quotations from classmates.", "Language: range of past tenses for narration and present tenses for reflection, vivid descriptive vocabulary."] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "You recently stayed at a youth hostel that markets itself as 'eco-friendly'. Write a text giving your opinion of the hostel for other young travellers. Choose one text type: review for a travel website, blog post, or email to a friend planning a trip. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a balanced evaluation with specific details (facilities, staff, location, green claims) leading to a clear recommendation; high C needs the conventions of the chosen type and a register suited to young travellers.", pts: ["Message: describes key aspects - location, rooms, food, atmosphere, staff - with concrete detail.", "Message: evaluates whether the eco-friendly claims are justified (solar power, refill stations, recycling, local produce).", "Message: balances strengths and weaknesses and ends with a clear recommendation or rating.", "Review conventions: title, name and location of the hostel, star rating, evaluative language, recommendation.", "Blog conventions: title, personal anecdotes, informal engaging voice, photos referred to, comments invited.", "Email conventions: greeting, friendly informal register, advice addressed to the friend, sign-off.", "Language: evaluative adjectives and comparatives used accurately (spotless, overpriced, more welcoming than)."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>The menu my parents wrote</b><p><b>[1]</b> My parents opened the Golden Lotus in 1994, two years after arriving with three suitcases and almost no English. The first menu, handwritten by my father, promised 'sweet and sour pork' and 'fried noodle special' - dishes they rarely ate themselves but which they believed local customers expected.</p><p><b>[2]</b> For years the restaurant survived on those safe choices. The food my grandmother cooked upstairs - bitter melon, fermented tofu, a soup that simmered all afternoon - stayed out of sight, as if it were slightly embarrassing.</p><p><b>[3]</b> When I took over the kitchen last year, I made one change: I put my grandmother's soup on the menu. My father predicted disaster. Instead, it became our best-selling dish, ordered as often by the builders next door as by homesick students.</p><p><b>[4]</b> I have thought a lot about why my parents hid that food. I think they were not ashamed of it so much as afraid of being rejected. When you arrive with nothing, fitting in can feel like survival. My generation can afford a luxury they could not: we can be curious instead of careful.</p></blockquote><p>(a) In which year did the writer's parents arrive in the country?</p><p>(b) Why did the first menu include dishes such as 'sweet and sour pork'?</p><p>(c) Find the word in paragraph 2 that means 'cooked gently, just below boiling'.</p><p>(d) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The writer's father was confident that the soup would be popular.'</p><p>(e) Which phrase in paragraph 3 shows that the soup appealed to different kinds of customer?</p><p>(f) According to paragraph 4, why did the parents hide the family's food?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) 1992 [1]", "(b) they believed local customers expected those dishes [1]", "(c) simmered [1]", "(d) False - 'My father predicted disaster' [1]", "(e) 'ordered as often by the builders next door as by homesick students' [1]", "(f) they were afraid of being rejected / fitting in felt like survival [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Money that migrants send to their families in their home countries is known as remittances. According to World Bank estimates, remittances to low- and middle-income countries reached around 650 billion US dollars in 2023 - considerably more than official development aid. For many households, this money pays for school fees, medicine and housing.</p></blockquote><p>Which statement is supported by the text?</p>", options: ["Remittances are smaller than foreign aid.", "Remittances mainly go to high-income countries.", "Remittances to low- and middle-income countries were larger than official development aid.", "The World Bank sends remittances to families."], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (Remittances to low- and middle-income countries were larger than official development aid.) - The text says remittances were 'considerably more than official development aid'."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>After eight years as a software engineer in Toronto, Ama returned to Accra to start her own company. 'Leaving was the easiest decision of my life,' she says. 'Coming back was the hardest.' She now employs twenty-three people, several of whom also studied or worked abroad.</p></blockquote><p>(a) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Ama found returning home easier than leaving.'</p><p>(b) How many people does Ama's company employ?</p><p>(c) Find the word in the text that means 'went back'.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) False - 'Coming back was the hardest' [1]", "(b) twenty-three / 23 [1]", "(c) returned [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (local radio interview)</i><p><b>Presenter:</b> Today I'm talking to Farida, who runs free conversation classes for newcomers to the city. Farida, why conversation rather than grammar?</p><p><b>Farida:</b> Because grammar books don't teach you how to ask a neighbour to turn the music down! Most of my learners already know some grammar. What they lack is confidence.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> And who does the teaching?</p><p><b>Farida:</b> Volunteers - mostly retired people, and a few students. We meet on Tuesday and Thursday evenings in the public library.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> What has been the biggest surprise?</p><p><b>Farida:</b> That the volunteers learn as much as the learners. One retired bus driver told me he had lived here for sixty years and had never had a conversation with a refugee before.</p></blockquote><p>(a) According to Farida, what do most of her learners lack?</p><p>(b) On which evenings do the classes take place?</p><p>(c) Where do the classes take place?</p><p>(d) What has surprised Farida most?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) confidence [1]", "(b) Tuesday and Thursday [1]", "(c) the public library [1]", "(d) the volunteers learn as much as the learners [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "A community magazine is publishing a series called 'Journeys' about local people who moved to the area from another country. Interview an older relative or neighbour (real or imagined) about their migration experience and write the text for the series. Choose one text type: interview, feature article, or blog post. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a coherent life story with reasons for leaving, challenges and reflections, using quotations or questions to bring the person's voice to life; high C needs the chosen type's conventions (an interview uses a Q&A layout with an introduction).", pts: ["Message: reasons for migrating (work, study, safety, family) and first impressions on arrival.", "Message: challenges - language, documents, homesickness, prejudice - and how they were overcome.", "Message: reflection on identity, belonging and advice for newcomers today.", "Interview conventions: title, introduction to the interviewee, clearly marked questions and answers, closing thanks.", "Feature article conventions: headline, standfirst, byline, quotations woven into the narrative, subheadings.", "Blog conventions: personal framing, title, direct address to readers, invitation to comment.", "Language: accurate past tenses and reported speech; vocabulary of migration (settle, homesick, residence permit)."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+2+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Never too late</b><p><b>[1]</b> My grandfather left school at eleven to work on his uncle's farm. For the next sixty years he hid a secret so well that even my grandmother only discovered it after they had been married for decades: he could barely read.</p><p><b>[2]</b> He had developed clever strategies. In restaurants he would order 'whatever you're having'. He memorised the shapes of road signs and the colours of medicine packets. When official forms arrived, he claimed to have forgotten his glasses.</p><p><b>[3]</b> At seventy, after my grandmother's death, he signed up for an adult literacy class at the local library. 'I was the oldest person in the room by thirty years,' he told me, 'and the most frightened.' His tutor, a retired teacher, began with the labels on his own tins and jars.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Two years later he read aloud at my sister's wedding. It was a short poem, and he stumbled twice. Nobody in the room will forget it. When I asked him why he had finally decided to learn, he shrugged: 'I got tired of pretending. Pretending is hard work.'</p></blockquote><p>(a) Why did the grandfather leave school?</p><p>(b) Identify two strategies he used to hide that he could not read well.</p><p>(c) In 'began with the labels on his own tins and jars' (paragraph 3), whose tins and jars are referred to?</p><p>(d) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'He was the youngest person in the literacy class.'</p><p>(e) Find the word in paragraph 4 that means 'made mistakes or hesitated while speaking'.</p><p>(f) According to the grandfather, why did he finally decide to learn?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) to work on his uncle's farm [1]", "(b) ordering 'whatever you're having' in restaurants [1]", "(b) memorising the shapes of road signs [1]", "(b) memorising the colours of medicine packets [1]", "(b) claiming to have forgotten his glasses [1]", "(c) the grandfather's [1]", "(d) False - 'I was the oldest person in the room by thirty years' [1]", "(e) stumbled [1]", "(f) he was tired of pretending / pretending is hard work [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>In many Latin American communities, a girl's fifteenth birthday is celebrated with a quinceañera, a party that traditionally marks her passage from childhood to womanhood. Celebrations may include a religious service, a formal waltz with her father and the symbolic change from flat shoes to high heels. Today, some families spend a year's savings on the event, while others choose a simple meal and put the money towards education.</p></blockquote><p>The change of shoes is described as 'symbolic' because it:</p>", options: ["is required by law", "represents the move into adulthood", "is the most expensive part of the party", "was borrowed from another culture"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (represents the move into adulthood) - A symbolic act stands for something else; here the heels stand for the passage from childhood to womanhood."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Japan celebrates Coming of Age Day on the second Monday of January, when young adults dress in formal kimono or suits and attend ceremonies organised by their local town halls. In April 2022 the legal age of adulthood was lowered from twenty to eighteen, yet many towns chose to keep inviting twenty-year-olds, partly because eighteen-year-olds are busy preparing for university entrance exams in January.</p></blockquote><p>(a) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Coming of Age Day falls on the same date every year.'</p><p>(b) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'All towns now hold the ceremony for eighteen-year-olds.'</p><p>(c) Give one reason why many towns did not invite eighteen-year-olds.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) False - 'the second Monday of January' [1]", "(b) False - 'many towns chose to keep inviting twenty-year-olds' [1]", "(c) eighteen-year-olds are busy preparing for university entrance exams [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your community is holding an evening to celebrate people who have made a difference to local life. Write a text about a person (real or imagined) who has strongly influenced your life. Choose one text type: speech for the evening, blog post, or letter to the person. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a vivid portrait supported by specific anecdotes and reflection on the person's influence; high C needs the conventions of the chosen type (a speech addresses an audience; a letter addresses the person directly).", pts: ["Message: introduces the person and the relationship, with concrete details of character.", "Message: two or three anecdotes that show the person's qualities rather than just listing them.", "Message: reflection on how the person changed the writer's values, choices or confidence.", "Speech conventions: greeting the audience, signposting, humour or emotion suited to a celebration, closing thanks or toast.", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, direct address to readers, invitation to share their own stories.", "Letter conventions: date, 'Dear...', warm personal register, closing such as 'With love' or 'With gratitude'.", "Language: narrative tenses, descriptive adjectives of character and expressions of gratitude used accurately."] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", t: "General conversation (theme: Experiences). The teacher asks: 'Are traditional rites of passage, such as graduation ceremonies or coming-of-age celebrations, still meaningful for young people today?' Outline how you would develop and sustain your answer.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B2 5-6 needs a developed, nuanced opinion with examples; C 5-6 needs sustained, spontaneous interaction and responses to follow-up questions.", pts: ["Clear opinion: ceremonies still matter because they mark change publicly and bring families together.", "Examples: graduation, religious ceremonies, quinceañera, Coming of Age Day, getting a driving licence or first job.", "Nuance: some rites have become commercial or expensive; new informal rites (first trip alone) may replace them.", "Personal or cultural connection: a rite of passage in the candidate's own family or culture.", "Interaction: asking the teacher's view, extending answers with reasons, asking for clarification when needed."] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-3": {
    units: { a: "Technology & communication", b: "Artistic expression", c: "Science & innovation" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Phones in lockers: one term on</b><p><b>[1]</b> When Westbrook Academy announced that phones would be locked away from the first bell to the last, students launched an online petition within hours. 'They treated it like the end of the world,' admits head teacher Dana Morrow. 'Ironically, the petition was organised entirely on phones.'</p><p><b>[2]</b> One term later, the mood has shifted. Teachers report that lunchtimes are noisier - in a good way. The library has started lending board games, and a chess club that once had four members now has forty.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Not everyone is convinced. Some parents complain that they can no longer reach their children during the day, although the school office passes on urgent messages. A group of older students argues that the policy treats them like children rather than teaching them to manage technology responsibly.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Morrow accepts that the criticism is fair. Next year, final-year students will be allowed to use phones in a designated study area. 'The aim was never to punish anyone,' she says. 'It was to give them back some time.'</p><p><i>Westbrook Academy and the people quoted are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) How quickly did students start their petition?</p><p>(b) Why does the head teacher describe the petition as ironic?</p><p>(c) Which phrase in paragraph 2 shows that the change in lunchtimes is positive?</p><p>(d) By how many members has the chess club grown?</p><p>(e) What is the parents' complaint?</p><p>(f) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The school refuses to make any changes to the policy.'</p><p>(g) In 'to give them back some time' (paragraph 4), who does 'them' refer to?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) within hours [1]", "(b) it was organised on phones, the very thing being banned [1]", "(c) 'in a good way' [1]", "(d) 36 (from four to forty) [1]", "(e) they can no longer reach their children during the day [1]", "(f) False - 'Next year, final-year students will be allowed to use phones in a designated study area' [1]", "(g) the students [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Deepfakes - videos in which artificial intelligence makes a person appear to say or do something they never did - are becoming harder to spot with the naked eye. Before sharing a shocking clip, ask yourself three questions: Who posted it first? Has a trusted news organisation reported it? Do the speaker's lip movements match the words exactly? If in doubt, don't share.</p></blockquote><p>What is the main purpose of this text?</p>", options: ["To explain how deepfake videos are made", "To warn readers about manipulated videos and advise them to check before sharing", "To advertise software that detects deepfakes", "To argue that artificial intelligence should be banned"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (To warn readers about manipulated videos and advise them to check before sharing) - The text defines deepfakes, warns that they are hard to spot and gives advice ('ask yourself three questions', 'If in doubt, don't share')."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Screen readers convert the text on a screen into synthetic speech or Braille, allowing blind and partially sighted people to use computers and phones. However, they only work well when websites are designed properly: an image without a written description, known as 'alt text', is simply announced as 'image', leaving the user guessing.</p></blockquote><p>Complete each sentence beginning (a)-(c) with the correct ending (A-E) according to the text. Write the letter and the full ending.</p><p>(a) Screen readers ...</p><p>(b) Alt text ...</p><p>(c) Badly designed websites ...</p><p>A. turn on-screen text into speech or Braille.<br>B. describes an image in words.<br>C. can leave screen-reader users guessing.<br>D. are used only by people who are completely blind.<br>E. make images load faster.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) A - screen readers turn on-screen text into speech or Braille [1]", "(b) B - alt text describes an image in words [1]", "(c) C - badly designed websites can leave screen-reader users guessing [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The world generated a record 62 million tonnes of electronic waste in 2022, according to a United Nations report, and less than a quarter of it was documented as properly collected and recycled. Old phones contain small amounts of gold, silver and copper, so throwing them away wastes valuable resources as well as risking pollution.</p></blockquote><p>Which <b>two</b> of the following statements are true according to the text?</p><p>A. The amount of e-waste reached a record level in 2022.<br>B. Most e-waste is properly recycled.<br>C. Old phones contain valuable metals.<br>D. Gold is the only metal found in phones.<br>E. The report was written by a phone company.</p>", options: ["A and B", "A and C", "C and D", "B and E"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (A and C) - A: 'a record 62 million tonnes ... in 2022'; C: phones contain 'gold, silver and copper'. B is wrong (less than a quarter recycled), D is wrong (three metals), E is wrong (a United Nations report)."] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school is considering replacing all printed textbooks with tablets next year. Write a text giving your views on the plan to the people who will make the decision. Choose one text type: formal letter to the principal, speech at a parents' evening, or article for the school newsletter. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear position, developed with advantages and disadvantages (cost, weight, distraction, eye strain, access at home) and a reasoned recommendation; high C needs a formal, respectful register and the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: clear stance on the plan, stated early and restated in the conclusion.", "Message: developed arguments - lighter bags, updated content, interactive features versus distraction, screen fatigue, cost and unequal internet access.", "Message: practical recommendation (for example a gradual trial, or keeping printed books for some subjects).", "Letter conventions: addresses, date, 'Dear Principal', clear purpose in the first paragraph, 'Yours sincerely' and full name.", "Speech conventions: greeting parents and staff, signposting, rhetorical questions, closing thanks.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph, subheadings, quotations from students or teachers.", "Language: formal register, hedging (it could be argued, there is a risk that) and accurate complex sentences."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>A piano in the station</b><p><b>[1]</b> Last spring the city's transport authority placed an old upright piano in the main hall of Central Station with a simple sign: 'Please play.' Officials expected it to last a month before vandals or complaints ended the experiment. Nine months later, it is still there.</p><p><b>[2]</b> The players are as varied as the passengers. A cleaner on her break plays hymns she learned as a child; a teenager in a football shirt hammers out film themes; a businessman in a suit once performed an entire sonata while his train left without him. Commuters who normally avoid eye contact stop, film and occasionally applaud.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Some notes are less welcome. Café owners nearby have asked for playing to be limited to daytime hours, and one regular passenger wrote to a newspaper complaining that 'beginners practising scales at 7 a.m. are not art, they are noise.'</p><p><b>[4]</b> For the project's organiser, that tension is part of the point. 'Art does not have to happen behind closed doors with expensive tickets,' she argues. 'Sometimes it is a stranger who is not very good, playing anyway.'</p></blockquote><p>(a) How long did officials expect the piano to last?</p><p>(b) Find the word in paragraph 2 that means 'people who travel regularly to and from work'.</p><p>(c) What happened to the businessman's train while he was playing?</p><p>(d) What have café owners asked for?</p><p>(e) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Everyone who uses the station enjoys the piano.'</p><p>(f) Which phrase in paragraph 4 suggests that art is often expensive or exclusive?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) a month [1]", "(b) Commuters [1]", "(c) it left / departed while he was still playing [1]", "(d) playing to be limited to daytime hours [1]", "(e) False - one passenger complained that 'beginners practising scales at 7 a.m. are not art, they are noise' [1]", "(f) 'behind closed doors with expensive tickets' [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><i>The Salt Orchard</i>, the first novel by Leila Haddad, follows three sisters trying to save a failing olive farm. The plot sags somewhat in the middle, where a subplot about a lost will goes nowhere, but the final fifty pages are so gripping that most readers will forgive it. Four stars.</p><p><i>The novel and author are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>Which statement best summarises the reviewer's opinion?</p>", options: ["The novel is disappointing from start to finish.", "The novel is good overall despite a weaker middle section.", "The reviewer could not finish the novel.", "Only the middle section of the novel is worth reading."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (The novel is good overall despite a weaker middle section.) - The reviewer criticises the middle ('sags') but praises the ending ('gripping') and gives four stars."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>In October 2018, moments after Banksy's painting <i>Girl with Balloon</i> was sold at auction in London for just over one million pounds, a shredder hidden in its frame sliced half of the canvas into strips. The artist renamed the damaged work <i>Love is in the Bin</i>. Three years later it was sold again for around 18.6 million pounds.</p></blockquote><p>(a) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The entire canvas was destroyed.'</p><p>(b) Where was the shredder hidden?</p><p>(c) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The damaged work lost value.'</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) False - 'sliced half of the canvas into strips' [1]", "(b) in the frame [1]", "(c) False - 'it was sold again for around 18.6 million pounds' [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (arts podcast)</i><p><b>Presenter:</b> Kofi, your mural on the side of the hospital is twelve metres high. How long did it take?</p><p><b>Kofi:</b> Six weeks of painting, but almost a year of planning. I interviewed nurses, cleaners and patients first, because I wanted the wall to tell their stories, not mine.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> So the faces in it are real people?</p><p><b>Kofi:</b> Yes - fourteen of them, all volunteers. The hardest part wasn't the height, it was the weather. It rained for nine days in the first fortnight.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> What do you hope people feel when they look at it?</p><p><b>Kofi:</b> That the people who look after them are seen.</p></blockquote><p>(a) How high is the mural?</p><p>(b) How long did Kofi spend planning the mural?</p><p>(c) Why did Kofi interview nurses, cleaners and patients?</p><p>(d) What was the hardest part of the project?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) twelve metres [1]", "(b) almost a year [1]", "(c) he wanted the wall to tell their stories [1]", "(d) the weather / the rain [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school plans to cut the budget for art, music and drama in order to buy more computers. Write a text arguing that the arts should keep their funding. Choose one text type: speech to the school board, formal letter to the principal, or opinion article for the school magazine. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a persuasive, developed argument (creativity, well-being, teamwork, careers in creative industries) that also responds to the case for computers; high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a respectful formal register.", pts: ["Message: clear position that arts funding should be protected, stated early.", "Message: developed reasons - creativity and problem-solving, mental health, confidence from performing, careers in design, film and music.", "Message: acknowledges the value of technology and proposes a compromise or alternative funding.", "Speech conventions: addressing the board, signposting, rhetorical devices (tricolon, rhetorical questions), closing appeal and thanks.", "Letter conventions: date, 'Dear...', purpose stated in the opening, formal close with name and role.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, engaging lead, subheadings, quotations from students.", "Language: persuasive structures and topic vocabulary (expression, imagination, well-being) used accurately."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "2+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Lettuce on the fourteenth floor</b><p><b>[1]</b> In a former office block in the port city of Lindmar, lettuce grows in stacked trays under purple LED lights. There is no soil, no sunlight and no rain. Instead, the roots hang in a mist of water and nutrients that is recycled continuously.</p><p><b>[2]</b> The company behind the farm, GreenStack, claims it uses about 95% less water than a conventional field and needs no pesticides, because insects cannot get in. As the farm is in the city centre, salads reach supermarket shelves within hours of harvest rather than days.</p><p><b>[3]</b> The technology is expensive, however. Lighting and climate control consume large amounts of electricity, and several vertical-farming companies elsewhere have gone out of business when energy prices rose. Critics also point out that leafy greens are easy to grow this way; wheat and rice, which feed most of the world, are much harder.</p><p><b>[4]</b> GreenStack's founder, Mila Varga, makes no claim to have solved world hunger. 'We are one tool, not the whole toolbox,' she says. 'But in a city that imports almost all its fresh food, growing some of it fourteen floors up makes sense.'</p><p><i>Lindmar, GreenStack and Mila Varga are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify two things the lettuce grows without, according to paragraph 1.</p><p>(b) Why are pesticides unnecessary on the farm?</p><p>(c) Find the word in paragraph 3 that means 'use up'.</p><p>(d) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Vertical farming is equally suitable for all major food crops.'</p><p>(e) What does Varga mean when she says the farm is 'one tool, not the whole toolbox'?</p><p>(f) Why does the farm make sense for Lindmar, according to Varga?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) soil [1]", "(a) sunlight [1]", "(a) rain [1]", "(b) insects cannot get in [1]", "(c) consume [1]", "(d) False - 'wheat and rice, which feed most of the world, are much harder' [1]", "(e) vertical farming is only one part of the solution to feeding people / it solves only part of the problem [1]", "(f) the city imports almost all its fresh food [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>In 1928 Alexander Fleming returned from a holiday to find that mould had contaminated one of his dishes of bacteria. Around the mould, the bacteria had died. Rather than throwing the dish away, he investigated, and the substance he identified, penicillin, became the basis of the first widely used antibiotic - although mass production began only in the 1940s, thanks to the work of other scientists.</p></blockquote><p>Which statement is supported by the text?</p>", options: ["Fleming planned the experiment carefully in advance.", "Fleming developed the mass production of penicillin on his own.", "An accidental observation led to a discovery that others helped to turn into a usable medicine.", "Penicillin was mass-produced in 1928."], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (An accidental observation led to a discovery that others helped to turn into a usable medicine.) - The mould 'contaminated' the dish (an accident), and mass production came later 'thanks to the work of other scientists'."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>A start-up in the coastal town of Port Aven is making food packaging from seaweed. Unlike conventional plastic, which can persist in the environment for hundreds of years, the seaweed film breaks down in a garden compost heap in about six weeks. Seaweed also grows quickly without fresh water or fertiliser, although harvesting it on a large scale could disturb marine habitats.</p><p><i>Port Aven is fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>Complete the following sentences with words taken from the text.</p><p>(a) The start-up's packaging is made from ...</p><p>(b) In a garden compost heap, the film breaks down in ...</p><p>(c) One possible risk of large-scale harvesting is that it could ...</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) seaweed [1]", "(b) about six weeks [1]", "(c) disturb marine habitats [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school's science club has designed a simple device that could solve an everyday problem in your community. A local company is offering funding for student inventions. Write a text to convince the company to fund your club's idea. Choose one text type: proposal, formal email, or presentation speech. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clearly described invention, the problem it solves, a realistic budget or plan, and benefits to the community; high C needs the conventions of the chosen type and a formal, confident register.", pts: ["Message: identifies a specific local problem and explains why it matters.", "Message: describes the device and how it works in clear, non-technical language.", "Message: practical details - costs, timeline, who will build and test it - and benefits for the company and community.", "Proposal conventions: title, To/From/Date/Subject, headings (Background, Proposal, Budget, Benefits), recommendation.", "Email conventions: subject line, formal greeting, concise paragraphs, polite request and sign-off.", "Speech conventions: greeting the panel, signposting, rhetorical questions, confident closing appeal.", "Language: precise technical and persuasive vocabulary (prototype, efficient, sustainable, cost-effective)."] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", t: "General conversation (theme: Human ingenuity). The teacher asks: 'Will artificial intelligence make human creativity less valuable?' Outline how you would develop and sustain your answer.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B2 5-6 needs a nuanced opinion developed with examples; C 5-6 needs spontaneous, sustained interaction and engagement with follow-up questions.", pts: ["Clear opinion: AI is a powerful tool, but human creativity still matters because of experience, emotion and intention.", "Examples: AI image and music generators, AI-assisted writing, designers using AI for drafts.", "Counter-argument: AI may replace some creative jobs and raise questions about copyright and originality.", "Personal or cultural connection: how the candidate or their school uses AI tools.", "Interaction: asking the teacher's opinion, extending answers with reasons, asking for clarification."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The James Webb Space Telescope was launched on 25 December 2021 and now orbits the Sun about 1.5 million kilometres from Earth. Its golden mirror, made of 18 hexagonal segments, was too large to fit inside the rocket in one piece, so it was folded for launch and unfolded in space. Because the telescope observes mainly infrared light, it can see through clouds of dust that block visible light.</p></blockquote><p>(a) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The telescope orbits the Earth.'</p><p>(b) Why was the mirror folded for launch?</p><p>(c) What does observing infrared light allow the telescope to do?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) False - 'now orbits the Sun about 1.5 million kilometres from Earth' [1]", "(b) it was too large to fit inside the rocket in one piece [1]", "(c) see through clouds of dust that block visible light [1]"] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-4": {
    units: { a: "Education & the working world", b: "Community & social engagement", c: "Law, order & media" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+2+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Friday off, every week</b><p><b>[1]</b> When the design agency Brightline told its forty employees they would work four days a week for the same salary, some clients assumed the firm was in financial trouble. In fact, it was an experiment. Staff would keep Fridays free, provided that productivity did not fall.</p><p><b>[2]</b> Six months on, the results are striking. Sick days have halved, and the agency has kept every one of its clients. Meetings, once the most hated part of the week, have been cut to thirty minutes and must have a written agenda.</p><p><b>[3]</b> The change has not suited everyone. Two employees say the compressed week leaves them exhausted by Thursday evening, and the agency's receptionist points out that customers keep calling on Fridays. A rota now ensures that someone is always available.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Economists caution that what works for a small creative firm may be unworkable for a hospital or a factory, where output depends on the hours staff are physically present. Still, Brightline's director has no plans to go back. 'We used to measure work by time,' she says. 'Now we measure it by results.'</p><p><i>Brightline is a fictional company.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) What condition was attached to the free Fridays?</p><p>(b) Identify two positive results of the experiment after six months.</p><p>(c) Find the word in paragraph 2 that means 'very noticeable or impressive'.</p><p>(d) What problem does the receptionist point out?</p><p>(e) How has the agency solved this problem?</p><p>(f) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Economists believe the four-day week will work in every workplace.'</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) productivity must stay the same / productivity did not fall [1]", "(b) sick days have halved [1]", "(b) the agency has kept all its clients [1]", "(b) meetings are shorter, cut to thirty minutes with a written agenda [1]", "(c) striking [1]", "(d) customers keep calling on Fridays [1]", "(e) a rota ensures someone is always available [1]", "(f) False - 'what works for a small creative firm may be unworkable for a hospital or a factory' [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Unpaid internships are often described as a 'foot in the door' to a career. Yet only young people whose families can support them can afford to work for months without pay, so critics say such schemes quietly favour those who are already privileged.</p></blockquote><p>The critics' main argument is that unpaid internships:</p>", options: ["teach no useful skills", "are unfair because mainly wealthier young people can afford them", "are against the law in most countries", "last too long to be useful"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (are unfair because mainly wealthier young people can afford them) - Only those 'whose families can support them' can afford them, so they 'favour those who are already privileged'."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>In Finland, children do not begin formal schooling until the age of seven, and there are no national standardised tests for pupils until the matriculation examination at the end of upper secondary school. Teachers are generally required to hold a master's degree, and the profession is highly respected.</p></blockquote><p>Which <b>two</b> of the following statements are true according to the text?</p><p>A. Finnish children start formal schooling at five.<br>B. There is a national examination at the end of upper secondary school.<br>C. Teachers are generally expected to have a master's degree.<br>D. Pupils take national tests every year.<br>E. Teaching is a poorly respected job in Finland.</p>", options: ["A and B", "B and D", "B and C", "C and E"], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (B and C) - B: 'the matriculation examination at the end of upper secondary school'; C: 'Teachers are generally required to hold a master's degree'. A is wrong (seven), D is wrong (no national tests before matriculation), E is wrong ('highly respected')."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (careers podcast)</i><p><b>Presenter:</b> Mr Osei, you've advised students for twenty years. What's the most common mistake in job interviews?</p><p><b>Mr Osei:</b> Failing to research the employer. If you can't say why you want to work there, the interview is over.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> What about nerves?</p><p><b>Mr Osei:</b> Nerves are fine - interviewers expect them. What they won't forgive is arriving late. Plan your route the day before.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> And at the end of the interview?</p><p><b>Mr Osei:</b> Always ask a question, something like 'What does a typical day look like?' It shows genuine interest.</p></blockquote><p>(a) According to Mr Osei, what is the most common mistake candidates make?</p><p>(b) What will interviewers not forgive?</p><p>(c) What does he advise candidates to do to avoid this?</p><p>(d) Why should candidates ask a question at the end of the interview?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) failing to research the employer [1]", "(b) arriving late [1]", "(c) plan your route the day before [1]", "(d) it shows genuine interest [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Many students in your school have part-time jobs alongside their studies. Write a text for younger students giving advice on how to balance paid work and schoolwork. Choose one text type: blog post, article for the school magazine, or set of guidelines. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs practical, well-organised advice with reasons and examples (benefits and risks of working, time management, rights at work); high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a supportive register suited to younger students.", pts: ["Message: benefits of a part-time job - money, independence, skills, references - balanced with risks such as tiredness and lower grades.", "Message: practical advice - limit hours, use a weekly planner, talk to employers about exam periods, know legal rights.", "Message: personal example or anecdote that makes the advice convincing.", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, direct address, questions to readers, invitation to comment.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph, subheadings, quotations from students.", "Guidelines conventions: clear title, numbered or bulleted points, imperatives, headings, summary.", "Language: modal verbs of advice (should, ought to, it is worth) and time expressions used accurately."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Borrow a drill, not a book</b><p><b>[1]</b> Most households own a power drill, yet it is often said that the average drill is used for only a few minutes in its entire life. That statistic, repeated so often that nobody remembers where it came from, inspired three neighbours in the town of Eastfield to open a 'library of things' in a disused shop.</p><p><b>[2]</b> Members pay a small annual fee - or nothing, if they cannot afford it - and can borrow anything from a tent to a sewing machine for a week. The most popular item is a karaoke machine, which is booked every weekend until the end of the year.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Volunteers check every item when it is returned and repair what they can. 'Things break; that's normal,' says co-founder Raj Mehta. 'What isn't normal is throwing them away the moment they do.'</p><p><b>[4]</b> The library has had an effect its founders did not predict. Borrowers chat while they wait, swap advice and recommend each other for odd jobs. 'We set out to share objects,' Mehta says. 'We ended up sharing skills and, honestly, friendships.'</p><p><i>Eastfield and the people quoted are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Why does the writer suggest that the statistic about drills may be unreliable?</p><p>(b) Find the word in paragraph 1 that means 'no longer used'.</p><p>(c) What happens if members cannot afford the annual fee?</p><p>(d) Which item is borrowed most often?</p><p>(e) In 'throwing them away the moment they do' (paragraph 3), what does 'they' refer to?</p><p>(f) According to paragraph 4, what unexpected effect has the library had?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) nobody remembers where it came from [1]", "(b) disused [1]", "(c) they pay nothing / they can borrow for free [1]", "(d) a karaoke machine [1]", "(e) things / the items that break [1]", "(f) people share skills and make friendships / borrowers chat and swap advice [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>When the storm cut power to half the village, it was the residents' group chat, rather than the council website, that told people where to find a generator, which elderly neighbours needed checking on and when the road would reopen. 'For years the chat was mainly lost cats and complaints about parking,' one member laughed. 'That night it became a lifeline.'</p></blockquote><p>In the final sentence, 'a lifeline' suggests that the group chat became:</p>", options: ["a place for complaints", "an essential source of help in an emergency", "a way of reporting lost pets", "a permanent replacement for the council"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (an essential source of help in an emergency) - A lifeline is something that gives vital help in a crisis; the chat organised generators, checks on elderly people and road information."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The idea of the 'third place' describes somewhere that is neither home (the first place) nor work or school (the second): a café, a park bench, a barber's shop or a library where people meet informally. Sociologists argue that such places build trust between neighbours. As high streets lose shops and many young people socialise mainly online, some fear that third places are disappearing.</p></blockquote><p>(a) According to the text, what are the 'first' and 'second' places?</p><p>(b) Give one example of a third place mentioned in the text.</p><p>(c) Identify one reason why some people fear third places are disappearing.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) home is the first place and work or school is the second [1]", "(b) a café / a park bench / a barber's shop / a library [1]", "(c) high streets are losing shops [1]", "(c) many young people socialise mainly online [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Many elderly residents in your town live alone and rarely leave their homes. Write a text proposing a scheme in which students and older people spend time together regularly. Choose one text type: proposal to the town council, article for the local newspaper, or speech at a community meeting. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear, practical scheme (activities, organisation, safety, benefits for both generations); high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a register suited to the audience.", pts: ["Message: describes the problem of isolation among elderly residents and why it matters.", "Message: explains the scheme - activities (tech help, shared meals, reading, gardening), frequency, organisation and safeguarding.", "Message: benefits for both generations - reduced loneliness, skills exchange, empathy, volunteering experience.", "Proposal conventions: title, To/From/Date/Subject, headings, objective tone, recommendations and request for approval.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph, quotations, subheadings.", "Speech conventions: greeting the audience, signposting, rhetorical questions, call to action and thanks.", "Language: formal register, conditional structures (if the council agreed...) and vocabulary of community (isolation, intergenerational)."] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", t: "General conversation (theme: Social organization). The teacher asks: 'Is social media making communities stronger or weaker?' Outline how you would develop and sustain your answer.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B2 5-6 needs a developed, balanced opinion with examples; C 5-6 needs sustained, spontaneous interaction including responses to follow-up questions.", pts: ["Clear opinion with reasons, for example that it strengthens some communities while weakening face-to-face contact.", "Positive examples: neighbourhood group chats in emergencies, fundraising, online support groups.", "Negative examples: misinformation, online arguments, less time in local 'third places'.", "Personal or cultural connection: the candidate's own school or neighbourhood groups.", "Interaction: asking the teacher's view, extending answers, asking for clarification when needed."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+2+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Facing the victim</b><p><b>[1]</b> At sixteen, Leo (not his real name) broke into a corner shop and stole cigarettes and cash. Instead of appearing in court, he was offered a place on a restorative justice programme. That meant sitting in a room with the shop's owner, Mrs Adeyemi, and listening.</p><p><b>[2]</b> 'I thought it would be easy - say sorry, go home,' Leo says. 'It was the hardest hour of my life.' Mrs Adeyemi described how she had stopped sleeping, how she now locked the door whenever she was alone, and how her granddaughter no longer wanted to help in the shop.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Supporters of restorative justice say that meetings like this help victims feel heard and make offenders understand the real consequences of their actions. Several studies have found that offenders who take part are somewhat less likely to reoffend, although results vary between programmes.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Critics argue that the approach can seem too soft, and that victims may feel pressured to forgive. Participation, however, is voluntary for both sides. Mrs Adeyemi says she agreed for one reason: 'I wanted him to see my face, not just my shop.'</p></blockquote><p>(a) What did Leo steal from the shop?</p><p>(b) Which phrase in paragraph 2 shows that the meeting was harder than Leo expected?</p><p>(c) Identify two effects the crime had on Mrs Adeyemi.</p><p>(d) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Research shows that restorative justice always reduces reoffending.'</p><p>(e) Find the word in paragraph 4 that means 'done by choice'.</p><p>(f) What did Mrs Adeyemi mean by 'I wanted him to see my face, not just my shop'?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) cigarettes and cash [1]", "(b) 'It was the hardest hour of my life' [1]", "(c) she stopped sleeping [1]", "(c) she locks the door whenever she is alone [1]", "(c) her granddaughter no longer wants to help in the shop [1]", "(d) False - 'results vary between programmes' [1]", "(e) voluntary [1]", "(f) she wanted him to understand that the crime hurt a real person / its human impact [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Newspapers that publish an error are expected to print a correction. Critics note, however, that corrections usually appear as tiny notices on an inside page, while the original mistake may have been a front-page headline seen by millions.</p></blockquote><p>The critics' main point is that corrections:</p>", options: ["are never published", "receive far less attention than the original error", "always appear on the front page", "are written by readers rather than journalists"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (receive far less attention than the original error) - Tiny notices on an inside page are seen by far fewer people than a front-page headline."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>In the city of Corvale, average-speed cameras were installed along a dangerous stretch of coastal road in 2021. Unlike fixed cameras, which drivers learn to slow down for and then speed up again, average-speed cameras calculate a vehicle's speed between two points several kilometres apart. In the three years after they were installed, the number of serious collisions on the road fell from fourteen to five, compared with the three years before.</p><p><i>Corvale is fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Complete the sentence with words from the text: Average-speed cameras calculate a vehicle's speed between ...</p><p>(b) According to the text, what do drivers often do after passing a fixed camera?</p><p>(c) By how many did the number of serious collisions fall?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) two points several kilometres apart [1]", "(b) speed up again [1]", "(c) 9 / nine collisions [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "A national television channel wants to broadcast criminal trials live, arguing that 'justice must be seen to be done'. Write a text giving your opinion on the plan. Choose one text type: letter to the editor of a national newspaper, opinion column, or speech for a school debate. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear position developed with arguments on both sides (transparency and public education versus privacy, pressure on witnesses, trials becoming entertainment); high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a formal register.", pts: ["Message: clear stance on televising trials, stated in the introduction.", "Message: arguments for - transparency, public trust, education about the legal system.", "Message: arguments against - pressure on witnesses and jurors, victims' privacy, trials turned into entertainment.", "Letter conventions: 'Dear Editor', reference to the channel's announcement, formal tone, name and town at the end.", "Opinion column conventions: headline, byline, provocative opening, rhetorical questions, strong conclusion.", "Debate speech conventions: addressing the chair and audience, stating the motion, rebutting the opposing side.", "Language: formal discursive connectors and legal vocabulary (defendant, witness, jury, verdict) used accurately."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Each year the organisation Reporters Without Borders publishes the World Press Freedom Index, which ranks 180 countries and territories according to how freely journalists can work. Norway has topped the index for several years in a row. The ranking is based partly on questionnaires completed by journalists, lawyers and researchers, and partly on recorded attacks against journalists.</p></blockquote><p>(a) How many countries and territories does the index rank?</p><p>(b) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The index is based only on recorded attacks against journalists.'</p><p>(c) Which country has been ranked first for several years?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) 180 [1]", "(b) False - 'based partly on questionnaires completed by journalists, lawyers and researchers' [1]", "(c) Norway [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (radio phone-in)</i><p><b>Presenter:</b> Sergeant Diaz, which online crime affects teenagers most?</p><p><b>Sgt Diaz:</b> Scams, believe it or not. Fake ticket sales for concerts are the big one.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> How can young people protect themselves?</p><p><b>Sgt Diaz:</b> Buy only from official sellers, and never pay a stranger by bank transfer. Once the money's gone, it's almost impossible to get back.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> And if they've already been tricked?</p><p><b>Sgt Diaz:</b> Tell an adult and report it straight away. Victims often stay silent because they feel embarrassed, and that's exactly what scammers rely on.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Which type of online crime affects teenagers most, according to Sergeant Diaz?</p><p>(b) Where should young people buy tickets?</p><p>(c) Why should they avoid paying strangers by bank transfer?</p><p>(d) Why do many victims stay silent?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) scams / fake ticket sales for concerts [1]", "(b) from official sellers [1]", "(c) once the money is gone it is almost impossible to get back [1]", "(d) they feel embarrassed [1]"] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-5": {
    units: { a: "Environment & climate", b: "Human rights & equality", c: "Globalization & ethics" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+2", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>The engineers with teeth</b><p><b>[1]</b> For centuries there were no wild beavers in the valley. Hunted for their fur, they had disappeared from Britain by around the sixteenth century. Last year, a pair was released into a fenced woodland at the top of the Ashdale valley.</p><p><b>[2]</b> Within months the animals had built three dams. The ponds behind them hold back water during heavy rain and release it slowly afterwards, which scientists believe could reduce flooding in the village downstream. The new wetlands have also attracted dragonflies, frogs and herons.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Some neighbours are less delighted. A farmer whose land borders the woodland worries that the beavers will escape and fell the trees in his orchard. 'Everyone in the city loves the idea of wildlife,' he says, 'until it is eating their apple trees.'</p><p><b>[4]</b> The project team has promised to protect valuable trees with wire mesh and to compensate any landowner who suffers damage. 'Rewilding only works if local people are partners rather than spectators,' says the project leader.</p><p><i>The Ashdale valley is fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Why did beavers disappear from Britain?</p><p>(b) How many dams did the beavers build?</p><p>(c) How might the ponds behind the dams reduce flooding?</p><p>(d) Find the word in paragraph 3 that means 'cut down (a tree)'.</p><p>(e) Which phrase in paragraph 3 suggests that city people may not understand the farmer's concern?</p><p>(f) Identify two promises made by the project team.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) they were hunted for their fur [1]", "(b) 3 / three dams [1]", "(c) they hold back water during heavy rain and release it slowly afterwards [1]", "(d) fell [1]", "(e) 'Everyone in the city loves the idea of wildlife until it is eating their apple trees' [1]", "(f) protect valuable trees with wire mesh [1]", "(f) compensate any landowner who suffers damage [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>'Buy less, choose well, make it last,' the fashion designer Vivienne Westwood famously advised. Her words have been adopted by a new generation of shoppers who swap clothes at clothing exchanges, rent outfits for special occasions and learn to mend holes rather than throw garments away.</p></blockquote><p>Which practice is <b>not</b> mentioned in the text?</p>", options: ["Swapping clothes", "Renting outfits for special occasions", "Mending damaged clothes", "Buying clothes made from recycled plastic"], answer: 3, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: D (Buying clothes made from recycled plastic) - Swapping, renting and mending are all mentioned; recycled-plastic clothing is not."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (science radio programme)</i><p><b>Presenter:</b> Dr Lind, you've measured the same Alpine glacier every summer for twelve years. What have you seen?</p><p><b>Dr Lind:</b> It has retreated by roughly four hundred metres. The ice cave where I took my first photograph no longer exists.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> Is that unusual?</p><p><b>Dr Lind:</b> Sadly, no. Almost every glacier in the Alps is shrinking.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> Why should this matter to people who never visit the mountains?</p><p><b>Dr Lind:</b> Because glaciers act like water towers. In dry summers, their meltwater keeps rivers flowing for farms and hydroelectric power stations far downstream.</p></blockquote><p>(a) For how long has Dr Lind measured the glacier?</p><p>(b) By how much has the glacier retreated?</p><p>(c) What has happened to the ice cave in her first photograph?</p><p>(d) Why does she compare glaciers to water towers?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) twelve years [1]", "(b) roughly four hundred metres [1]", "(c) it has disappeared / it is gone [1]", "(d) in dry summers their meltwater keeps rivers flowing for farms and power stations [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "After a long heatwave last summer, your region suffered serious water shortages. Write a text to persuade local residents to use less water at home. Choose one text type: leaflet, speech for local radio, or social media post. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs persuasive reasons and concrete, practical tips (shorter showers, fixing leaks, collecting rainwater, watering gardens in the evening) with a clear call to action; high C needs the conventions of the chosen type and a register suited to the general public.", pts: ["Message: explains the problem - heatwaves, low reservoirs, consequences for farms, wildlife and households.", "Message: practical tips - shorter showers, turning off taps, fixing leaks, full washing machines, collecting rainwater.", "Message: motivating appeal - community responsibility, saving money, protecting the future - and a call to action.", "Leaflet conventions: title and slogan, headings, bullet points, facts in boxes, contact details or website.", "Radio speech conventions: greeting listeners, short sentences, repetition, direct address, memorable closing line.", "Social media post conventions: eye-catching opening, hashtags, short paragraphs, link or tag, call to share.", "Language: imperatives, persuasive devices and environmental vocabulary (drought, reservoir, conserve) used accurately."] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", t: "General conversation (theme: Sharing the planet). The teacher asks: 'Should richer countries do more than poorer countries to reduce carbon emissions?' Outline how you would develop and sustain your answer.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B2 5-6 needs a developed, balanced opinion with relevant examples; C 5-6 needs sustained, spontaneous interaction and engagement with follow-up questions.", pts: ["Clear opinion with reasons: historical responsibility, wealth and technology of richer countries.", "Examples: international climate agreements such as the Paris Agreement, climate finance for developing countries.", "Counter-argument: fast-growing economies also produce large emissions, so all countries must act.", "Personal or cultural connection: what the candidate's own country or school is doing.", "Interaction: inviting the teacher's view, extending answers with examples, asking for clarification."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Hands up for sign language</b><p><b>[1]</b> When Maya was born deaf, one doctor told her parents that sign language would 'stop her learning to speak'. Today that view is widely rejected: research suggests that early access to a full language, whether signed or spoken, supports a child's development, and many deaf children use both.</p><p><b>[2]</b> Maya, now fourteen, attends a mainstream school with a sign-language interpreter. Last year her classmates asked whether they could learn too. The school agreed to an after-school club, expecting a handful of students. Ninety signed up.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Campaigners say Maya's experience is still the exception. In many countries deaf children have no access to qualified interpreters, and some national sign languages are still not legally recognised. The UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities calls on states to recognise and promote the use of sign languages.</p><p><b>[4]</b> For Maya, the club has changed more than communication. 'Before, people spoke to my interpreter instead of me,' she says. 'Now they look at me.'</p><p><i>Maya is a fictional name.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) What did one doctor claim about sign language?</p><p>(b) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Most experts today agree with the doctor.'</p><p>(c) How many students joined the after-school club?</p><p>(d) Which phrase in paragraph 2 shows that the school expected only a few students?</p><p>(e) Find the word in paragraph 3 that means 'something that is unusual or does not follow the rule'.</p><p>(f) What does the UN Convention ask states to do?</p><p>(g) What change does Maya describe in paragraph 4?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) it would stop her learning to speak [1]", "(b) False - 'Today that view is widely rejected' [1]", "(c) ninety / 90 [1]", "(d) 'a handful of students' [1]", "(e) exception [1]", "(f) recognise and promote the use of sign languages [1]", "(g) people now look at her and talk to her directly, rather than to her interpreter [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>In 2014, at the age of seventeen, Malala Yousafzai became the youngest person ever to receive the Nobel Peace Prize, which she shared with the Indian children's rights campaigner Kailash Satyarthi. Two years earlier she had survived being shot by the Taliban for speaking out in favour of girls' education in Pakistan.</p></blockquote><p>Which statement is correct according to the text?</p>", options: ["Malala received the prize alone.", "Malala was the youngest person ever to win the prize.", "Malala campaigned against girls' education.", "Kailash Satyarthi was from Pakistan."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (Malala was the youngest person ever to win the prize.) - The text says she 'became the youngest person ever to receive the Nobel Peace Prize'."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted by the United Nations General Assembly in Paris on 10 December 1948. Its thirty articles set out rights such as freedom from torture and the right to education. The committee that drafted it was chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt, and 10 December is now celebrated every year as Human Rights Day.</p></blockquote><p>Complete the following sentences with words taken from the text.</p><p>(a) The Declaration was adopted in the city of ...</p><p>(b) The Declaration is made up of ...</p><p>(c) Human Rights Day is celebrated every year on ...</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) Paris [1]", "(b) thirty articles [1]", "(c) 10 December [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The Paralympic movement grew out of a sports competition organised in 1948 by the neurologist Ludwig Guttmann for injured Second World War veterans at Stoke Mandeville Hospital in England. The first official Paralympic Games were held in Rome in 1960, and since 1988 the Summer Paralympics have taken place in the same city as the Olympic Games, shortly after them.</p></blockquote><p>(a) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The movement began with a competition for patients at a hospital.'</p><p>(b) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The first official Paralympic Games were held in England.'</p><p>(c) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Since 1988 the Summer Paralympics have been held before the Olympic Games.'</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) True - 'organised in 1948 by the neurologist Ludwig Guttmann for injured Second World War veterans at Stoke Mandeville Hospital' [1]", "(b) False - 'The first official Paralympic Games were held in Rome in 1960' [1]", "(c) False - 'in the same city as the Olympic Games, shortly after them' [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "At your school, the girls' sports teams receive less funding, fewer training hours and older equipment than the boys' teams. Write a text arguing for equal treatment. Choose one text type: formal letter to the principal, speech at a student council meeting, or article for the school magazine. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs specific evidence of the inequality, developed arguments for change and practical proposals; high C needs a formal, respectful register and the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: describes the inequality with specific, realistic details (budget, pitch time, kit, travel to matches).", "Message: explains why it matters - fairness, health, confidence, role models, school reputation.", "Message: proposes practical solutions (shared timetable, equal budget, review panel) and anticipates objections.", "Letter conventions: date, 'Dear Principal', purpose stated clearly, formal close and full name.", "Speech conventions: greeting the council, signposting, rhetorical questions, call to action and thanks.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph, subheadings, quotations from players.", "Language: formal register, persuasive structures and vocabulary of equality (equitable, discrimination, opportunity)."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Where your old clothes go</b><p><b>[1]</b> You drop a bag of old T-shirts into a charity bin and feel virtuous. But many donated clothes are never sold in the country where they were given. Instead, they are pressed into bales and shipped abroad - often to markets such as Kantamanto in Accra, Ghana, one of the largest second-hand clothing markets in the world.</p><p><b>[2]</b> Traders buy the bales without being allowed to see inside them. A good bale can feed a family for a month; a bad one, full of stained or torn clothes, can mean losing money. Locally, the clothes are known as 'obroni wawu' - 'dead white man's clothes' - a name said to date from a time when people assumed someone must have died for so many clothes to be given away.</p><p><b>[3]</b> According to one estimate by an organisation working in the market, around 40% of the clothing in each bale leaves the market as waste. Much of it ends up in landfills or washed up on beaches.</p><p><b>[4]</b> Some campaigners argue that wealthy countries should take responsibility for the clothes they export. Others point out that the trade supports tens of thousands of jobs. Both sides agree on one thing: the simplest solution is for consumers to buy fewer, better clothes.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Find the word in paragraph 1 that means 'morally good'.</p><p>(b) What happens to many donated clothes that are not sold locally?</p><p>(c) Why is buying a bale risky for traders?</p><p>(d) According to paragraph 2, where does the name 'obroni wawu' come from?</p><p>(e) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Nearly all the clothing in each bale is sold.'</p><p>(f) On which point do both sides of the debate agree?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) virtuous [1]", "(b) they are pressed into bales and shipped abroad [1]", "(c) traders cannot see inside the bales, so a bad bale full of stained or torn clothes means losing money [1]", "(d) people assumed someone must have died for so many clothes to be given away [1]", "(e) False - 'around 40% of the clothing in each bale leaves the market as waste' [1]", "(f) consumers should buy fewer, better clothes [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Strawberries bought in winter in northern Europe may have travelled thousands of kilometres. However, researchers warn that 'food miles' alone can mislead: tomatoes grown in heated greenhouses nearby can have a larger carbon footprint than tomatoes grown outdoors in a sunnier country and transported by lorry.</p></blockquote><p>What is the writer's main point?</p>", options: ["Locally grown food is always better for the environment.", "The distance food travels is only one factor in its environmental impact.", "Strawberries should not be sold in winter.", "Greenhouses produce no carbon emissions."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (The distance food travels is only one factor in its environmental impact.) - 'Food miles alone can mislead' - heating a local greenhouse can outweigh transport emissions."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Before the 1950s, cargo was loaded onto ships by hand, piece by piece, and a vessel could spend more time in port than at sea. In 1956 the American businessman Malcolm McLean sent a converted tanker carrying 58 metal containers from New Jersey to Texas. Standard-sized containers, which can move between ship, train and lorry without being unpacked, dramatically reduced the cost of shipping goods - and helped make today's global supply chains possible.</p></blockquote><p>Which <b>two</b> of the following statements are true according to the text?</p><p>A. Before the 1950s, cargo was loaded onto ships by hand.<br>B. McLean's first container ship carried 580 containers.<br>C. Containers must be unpacked when they move from a ship to a train.<br>D. Containers greatly reduced the cost of shipping goods.<br>E. Containers were invented in Texas.</p>", options: ["A and C", "B and D", "A and D", "D and E"], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (A and D) - A: 'loaded onto ships by hand, piece by piece'; D: containers 'dramatically reduced the cost of shipping goods'. B is wrong (58), C is wrong ('without being unpacked'), E is wrong (the ship sailed from New Jersey to Texas; Texas is the destination)."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The Swedish word <i>flygskam</i>, meaning 'flight shame', spread around the world in 2019 as some travellers chose trains over planes for environmental reasons. Rail companies in several European countries reported rising demand for long-distance and night trains, while airlines responded by promoting carbon-offset schemes, in which passengers pay extra to fund projects such as tree planting.</p></blockquote><p>(a) From which language does the word <i>flygskam</i> come?</p><p>(b) In carbon-offset schemes, what do passengers pay extra for?</p><p>(c) Find the word in the text that means 'reacted'.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) Swedish [1]", "(b) to fund projects such as tree planting [1]", "(c) responded [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school is choosing a new supplier for its uniforms. One offer is cheaper, but there are reports that the factory pays very low wages; the other is more expensive but certified as ethically produced. Write a text persuading the school to choose the ethical supplier. Choose one text type: proposal to the school leadership, formal letter to the principal, or article for the school newsletter. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs developed ethical and practical arguments (workers' rights, school values, quality, reputation) and a realistic response to the cost problem; high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a formal register.", pts: ["Message: explains the choice between the two suppliers and the ethical issue clearly.", "Message: arguments - workers' rights and fair wages, consistency with school values, quality and durability, reputation.", "Message: addresses cost with practical solutions (second-hand uniform sales, phased change, support for families).", "Proposal conventions: title, To/From/Date/Subject, headings, objective tone, clear recommendation.", "Letter conventions: date, 'Dear Principal', purpose in the first paragraph, formal close and name.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph, subheadings, quotations from students or staff.", "Language: formal register, persuasive structures and vocabulary of ethics (exploitation, fair wage, supply chain)."] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", t: "General conversation (theme: Sharing the planet). The teacher asks: 'Does globalization threaten local cultures, or does it help them reach the world?' Outline how you would develop and sustain your answer.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B2 5-6 needs a developed, balanced opinion with examples; C 5-6 needs sustained, spontaneous interaction and responses to follow-up questions.", pts: ["Clear opinion, for example that globalization does both, depending on how communities respond.", "Threat: global chains replacing local shops, English replacing local languages, identical high streets.", "Opportunity: local music, food and film reaching global audiences through streaming and social media.", "Personal or cultural connection: an example from the candidate's own city or country.", "Interaction: asking the teacher's view, extending answers with examples, asking for clarification."] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-6": {
    units: { a: "Personal texts", b: "Professional texts", c: "Register & audience" },
    paper: "P1",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+2+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><i>Thursday 14 March</i></p><p>I can't believe I actually did it. After three weeks of practising in front of the mirror, I stood up in assembly today and read my poem about Grandpa. My hands were shaking so much that the paper rattled into the microphone - everyone must have heard it. But halfway through, something strange happened: I stopped noticing the four hundred faces and just thought about him.</p><p>When I finished, there was a silence that felt like forever, and then Ms Ruiz started clapping. I'm still unsure whether I'm proud or embarrassed. Probably both. Tomorrow I'll ask Mum if I can send the poem to Grandma.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify the text type.</p><p>(b) Identify two features of the text that show its type.</p><p>(c) Which phrase shows that the writer was nervous at the start?</p><p>(d) In 'I actually did it', what does 'it' refer to?</p><p>(e) Which sentence shows that the writer has mixed feelings?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) diary entry / journal entry [1]", "(b) the date at the top [1]", "(b) intimate first-person voice sharing private feelings [1]", "(b) no reader is addressed / written for the writer alone [1]", "(b) reflective comments such as 'Probably both' [1]", "(c) 'My hands were shaking so much that the paper rattled into the microphone' [1]", "(d) reading the poem in assembly [1]", "(e) 'I'm still unsure whether I'm proud or embarrassed' [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Hi Sam! Sorry it's taken me ages to reply - exams have completely eaten my life. How's the new flat?</p></blockquote><p>Which sentence would be the most appropriate continuation of this email?</p>", options: ["I am writing to inform you of my intention to visit your city.", "Anyway, guess what - I'm coming to visit in July!", "Further to your correspondence of 3 May, please find my reply below.", "I would be grateful for a prompt response at your earliest convenience."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (Anyway, guess what - I'm coming to visit in July!) - The email is informal (contractions, 'ages', exclamation), so only the chatty option keeps the same register."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "2+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>OK, confession time: I have bought exactly zero new clothes this year. Zero! And honestly? It's been kind of amazing. Here's how I did it (and why you might want to try too)...</p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify two features of informal register in this blog opening, quoting an example of each.</p><p>(b) What is the purpose of the final sentence?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) contractions, e.g. 'It's' / 'Here's' [1]", "(a) exclamation, e.g. 'Zero!' [1]", "(a) rhetorical question, e.g. 'And honestly?' [1]", "(a) colloquial language, e.g. 'kind of amazing' / 'OK, confession time' [1]", "(a) direct address to readers, e.g. 'you might want to try too' [1]", "(b) to make readers keep reading / to introduce the advice that follows [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Last weekend your parents were away and you were responsible for looking after your two younger siblings. Write a text about the experience and what it taught you. Choose one text type: diary entry, blog post, or email to a friend. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a lively account with specific incidents and genuine reflection on responsibility; high C needs the chosen type's conventions and an informal register appropriate to the reader (oneself, online readers or a friend).", pts: ["Message: narrates specific incidents (cooking disaster, bedtime battle, a small emergency handled well).", "Message: reflects on what was learned - patience, responsibility, new respect for parents.", "Message: clear structure from expectations to outcome, with a humorous or thoughtful ending.", "Diary conventions: date, intimate first person, private emotions, no audience address.", "Blog conventions: title, direct address to readers, anecdotes, invitation to comment.", "Email conventions: greeting, friendly informal register, questions to the friend, sign-off.", "Language: narrative tenses (past simple, past continuous, past perfect) and vivid informal vocabulary used accurately."] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "You are about to leave home to study in another city. Write a text reflecting on what you will miss and what you hope for in the next stage of your life. Choose one text type: personal letter to a family member, diary entry, or blog post. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs reflection supported by specific memories and realistic hopes and fears; high C needs a personal register and the conventions of the chosen type (a letter addresses the family member directly).", pts: ["Message: specific things and people the writer will miss, with memories that bring them to life.", "Message: hopes and worries about independence, studies and new friendships.", "Message: reflective conclusion linking past and future.", "Personal letter conventions: date, 'Dear...', warm personal register, questions to the reader, closing such as 'Love'.", "Diary conventions: date, intimate first person, emotional reflection.", "Blog conventions: title, direct address to readers, invitation to share experiences.", "Language: range of tenses (past memories, present feelings, future hopes) and emotive vocabulary used accurately."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+2+1+2", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>Subject:</b> Enquiry about summer volunteer positions</p><p>Dear Ms Novak,</p><p>I am writing to enquire whether the Riverside Wildlife Centre will be offering volunteer positions this summer. I am a seventeen-year-old student with a keen interest in conservation, and last year I completed a two-week placement at a local animal shelter.</p><p>I would be grateful if you could let me know the dates of any programmes, the minimum age requirement and whether accommodation is provided. I have attached my CV for your reference.</p><p>Thank you for your time. I look forward to hearing from you.</p><p>Yours sincerely,<br>Daniel Ferreira</p><p><i>The centre and people are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) What is the main purpose of the email?</p><p>(b) Identify two pieces of information that Daniel requests.</p><p>(c) Which phrase shows that Daniel has relevant experience?</p><p>(d) Identify two conventions of a formal email used in the text.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) to enquire about / ask about summer volunteer positions [1]", "(b) the dates of programmes [1]", "(b) the minimum age requirement [1]", "(b) whether accommodation is provided [1]", "(c) 'last year I completed a two-week placement at a local animal shelter' [1]", "(d) subject line [1]", "(d) formal greeting with title and surname, 'Dear Ms Novak' [1]", "(d) formal closing 'Yours sincerely' with full name [1]", "(d) polite formulas such as 'I would be grateful if' or 'I look forward to hearing from you' [1]", "(d) reference to an attachment (CV) [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "Which sentence would be the most appropriate final sentence of a proposal to a school principal?", options: ["Anyway, hope you like it!", "I therefore recommend that the scheme be approved for a trial period beginning in September.", "Thanks loads for reading this far.", "That's all from me - see you around."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (I therefore recommend that the scheme be approved for a trial period beginning in September.) - A proposal ends with a formal, clear recommendation; the other options are informal."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>NOTICE TO RESIDENTS: WATER SUPPLY INTERRUPTION</b></p><p>Due to essential repairs, the water supply to Elm Road and Station Street will be switched off on Tuesday 9 April between 09:00 and 15:00.</p><p>Residents are advised to store sufficient drinking water in advance. Bottled water will be available from the community centre for elderly or disabled residents.</p><p>For further information, please contact the council helpline.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Why will the water supply be switched off?</p><p>(b) For how many hours will the water be switched off?</p><p>(c) What are residents advised to do before the interruption?</p><p>(d) Who can collect bottled water from the community centre?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) essential repairs [1]", "(b) six hours (09:00 to 15:00) [1]", "(c) store sufficient drinking water in advance [1]", "(d) elderly or disabled residents [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school council surveyed 300 students about the school library. The survey found that 65% of students rarely use it, mainly because of short opening hours, a lack of quiet study space and an outdated book collection. Write a text presenting these findings and recommending improvements. Choose one text type: report for the school leadership, proposal, or formal email to the head librarian. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs the findings presented clearly and linked logically to practical, justified recommendations; high C needs an objective formal register and the conventions of the chosen type.", pts: ["Message: purpose of the survey and method (300 students, questionnaire) stated clearly.", "Message: key findings - 65% rarely use the library; reasons: opening hours, quiet space, outdated books.", "Message: specific recommendations linked to each finding (longer hours, silent zone, student book requests, e-books).", "Report conventions: title, introduction/purpose, findings and recommendations under headings, impersonal style, conclusion.", "Proposal conventions: To/From/Date/Subject, headings, costs or timeline, request for approval.", "Email conventions: subject line, formal greeting, concise paragraphs, polite closing and name.", "Language: impersonal and passive structures (it was found that, students were asked) and accurate reporting of figures."] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "You would like to work as an assistant at a children's holiday camp this summer. Write a text applying for the position and explaining why you are suitable. Choose one text type: cover letter, formal email, or personal statement for an online application form. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs relevant qualities supported by concrete evidence (experience with children, skills, reliability) and an explanation of motivation; high C needs a formal, confident register and the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: states the position applied for and where it was advertised.", "Message: relevant experience and skills with examples (babysitting, coaching, first aid, languages).", "Message: motivation for the job and availability, ending with a request for an interview.", "Cover letter conventions: addresses, date, 'Dear...', clear paragraphs, 'Yours sincerely' and full name.", "Formal email conventions: subject line, formal greeting, concise paragraphs, attachment mentioned, sign-off.", "Personal statement conventions: first person, structured paragraphs, focus on qualities and goals, no greeting.", "Language: formal register, accurate present perfect for experience (I have worked...), positive vocabulary (reliable, enthusiastic)."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "2+2", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>Text A</b> (message to a classmate): Hey Lina, can't make the museum trip tomorrow - got a nasty cold :( Can you grab me one of those worksheets? Owe you one!</p><p><b>Text B</b> (email to a teacher): Dear Mr Haddad, I am writing to inform you that I will be unable to attend tomorrow's museum visit, as I am currently unwell. I would be grateful if you could let me know how I might complete the related assignment. Yours sincerely, Omar Said</p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify two features of informal register in Text A, quoting an example of each.</p><p>(b) Identify two features of formal register in Text B, quoting an example of each.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) informal greeting 'Hey' [1]", "(a) omitted subject / ellipsis, e.g. 'can't make' or 'got a nasty cold' [1]", "(a) emoticon ':(' [1]", "(a) colloquial expressions, e.g. 'grab' or 'Owe you one!' [1]", "(b) formal greeting and closing, 'Dear Mr Haddad' / 'Yours sincerely' [1]", "(b) full forms rather than contractions, e.g. 'I am writing' [1]", "(b) polite formula 'I would be grateful if you could' [1]", "(b) formal vocabulary, e.g. 'unable to attend' or 'currently unwell' [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "A formal letter that begins 'Dear Sir or Madam' should normally end with:", options: ["Yours faithfully", "Best wishes", "Lots of love", "See you soon"], answer: 0, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: A (Yours faithfully) - When the recipient's name is unknown, British convention is 'Dear Sir or Madam' ... 'Yours faithfully'."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Oh wonderful, another meeting that could have been an email.</p></blockquote><p>Which word best describes the tone of this sentence?</p>", options: ["Sincere", "Sarcastic", "Formal", "Sympathetic"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (Sarcastic) - 'Oh wonderful' says the opposite of what the speaker means: the speaker is annoyed, so the tone is sarcastic."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Ladies and gentlemen, distinguished guests, it is an honour to welcome you to the opening of our new Science Wing. Three years ago this site was a car park. Today it holds four laboratories, a rooftop greenhouse and, I am told, the most expensive coffee machine in the district. All of this was made possible by the generosity of our parents' association, and I would ask you all to join me in thanking them.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify the text type.</p><p>(b) Identify one feature that shows the register is formal.</p><p>(c) Which phrase is intended to make the audience laugh?</p><p>(d) What does the speaker ask the audience to do at the end?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) speech [1]", "(b) formal address 'Ladies and gentlemen, distinguished guests' / 'it is an honour to welcome you' [1]", "(c) 'the most expensive coffee machine in the district' [1]", "(d) join in thanking the parents' association [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your class wants to organise a three-day field trip to study a national park, but many families cannot afford the full cost. Write a text asking the parents' association to help fund the trip. Choose one text type: formal letter, proposal, or speech at a parents' association meeting. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear request supported by educational benefits, a realistic budget and fundraising plans; high C needs a polite, formal register suited to adult readers and the conventions of the chosen type.", pts: ["Message: explains the trip - destination, dates, learning aims and links to the curriculum.", "Message: sets out costs and the amount requested, plus students' own fundraising efforts.", "Message: explains the benefits for all students, especially those who could not otherwise attend, and thanks the association.", "Letter conventions: date, 'Dear members of the Parents' Association', clear purpose, formal close and name.", "Proposal conventions: title, To/From/Date/Subject, headings (Aims, Budget, Benefits), recommendation.", "Speech conventions: greeting the audience, signposting, polite persuasive appeal, thanks.", "Language: formal polite structures (we would be most grateful, we hope you will consider) used accurately."] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school is organising a reading week at a nearby primary school for children aged 8 to 10. Write a text to encourage the children to read for pleasure. Choose one text type: speech to the children, leaflet, or post for the primary school's website. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs ideas and examples that would appeal to young children (adventure, humour, favourite characters) and simple practical tips; high C needs a register clearly adapted to children aged 8-10 and the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: reasons to read that matter to children - adventure, laughter, imagination, learning about animals or space.", "Message: practical tips - choosing books, library visits, reading with family, reading challenges.", "Message: enthusiastic call to action for the reading week's activities.", "Speech conventions: friendly greeting, questions to the audience, simple repetition, enthusiastic close.", "Leaflet conventions: catchy title, headings, bullet points, short sentences, practical details.", "Website post conventions: title, short paragraphs, direct address, information about the week.", "Language: simple, clear vocabulary and short sentences that remain accurate and lively, avoiding complex jargon."] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-7": {
    units: { a: "News & feature articles", b: "Persuasive media", c: "Digital media" },
    paper: "P1",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>Teen inventor's flood alarm wins national prize</b></p><p>A 16-year-old student from the coastal town of Merrow has won a national young engineers' award for a low-cost alarm that warns residents when river levels rise.</p><p>Ana Petrov, who designed the device after her family's home was flooded in 2022, received the 5,000-euro prize at a ceremony in the capital on Friday. The alarm, built from recycled phone parts, sends a text message to subscribers when the water reaches a set height.</p><p>'I just wanted to make sure no other family wakes up to water in the kitchen,' Ana said. The judges praised the invention as 'simple, cheap and genuinely life-saving'.</p><p>The local council has announced that twenty alarms will be installed along the river this winter.</p><p><i>Merrow and Ana Petrov are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify the text type.</p><p>(b) Which part of the text summarises the main facts of the story in one sentence?</p><p>(c) Who won the prize?</p><p>(d) Why did she design the device?</p><p>(e) What material was the alarm built from?</p><p>(f) What will happen this winter?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) news report / news article [1]", "(b) the lead / the first paragraph [1]", "(c) Ana Petrov, a 16-year-old student [1]", "(d) her family's home was flooded in 2022 [1]", "(e) recycled phone parts [1]", "(f) twenty alarms will be installed along the river [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "The first paragraph of a news report is called the lead. Which is the most effective lead for a report on a school charity run?", options: ["Running is a sport that many people enjoy all around the world.", "More than 300 students from Hillcrest School ran 5 km through the town centre on Saturday, raising 6,200 euros for the local children's hospital.", "I have always loved charity events, and this one was definitely the best.", "In conclusion, the run was a great success for everyone involved."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (More than 300 students from Hillcrest School ran 5 km through the town centre on Saturday, raising 6,200 euros for the local children's hospital.) - A lead answers the key Ws (who, what, where, when, why) concisely and impersonally."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "2+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>The last bookbinder on Mill Street</b></p><p>The bell above the door still rings the way it did in 1971. Inside, the air smells of glue and leather, and Ezra Cohen, 82, is bent over a Bible so old that its pages crackle like autumn leaves. 'People bring me their grandmother's recipe books, their school prizes, their love letters,' he says, without looking up. 'They don't bring me books. They bring me memories.'</p><p><i>Ezra Cohen is a fictional character.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) This is the opening of a feature article rather than a news report. Identify two features that show this.</p><p>(b) Which phrase appeals to the sense of smell?</p><p>(c) What does Cohen mean when he says 'They bring me memories'?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) descriptive, scene-setting opening rather than a summary of facts [1]", "(a) sensory details and imagery, e.g. the simile 'pages crackle like autumn leaves' [1]", "(a) focus on a person / human-interest angle [1]", "(a) quotations used to reveal character [1]", "(b) 'the air smells of glue and leather' [1]", "(c) the books matter to people for their emotional or personal value [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "A local magazine is running a series on jobs that are disappearing. Write a text about a person in your community who does such a job (for example a shoe repairer, watchmaker or newspaper seller). Choose one text type: feature article, interview, or blog post. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a vivid portrait of the person and the job, with quotations, descriptive detail and reflection on why the job is disappearing; high C needs the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: descriptive portrait of the person and workplace using sensory details.", "Message: the history of the job and why it is disappearing (technology, cheap replacements, online shopping).", "Message: reflection on what the community loses and whether the skill can survive.", "Feature article conventions: headline, standfirst, byline, descriptive opening, quotations, subheadings.", "Interview conventions: introduction, clearly marked questions and answers, closing thanks.", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, direct address to readers, invitation to comment.", "Language: descriptive vocabulary, imagery and accurate use of reported and direct speech."] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school recently held a charity event that was a great success. Write a text reporting on the event for people who were unable to attend. Choose one text type: news report for the school website, blog post, or newsletter email to parents. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear account of the event (what, when, who, results) with quotations and details; high C needs the conventions of the chosen type (a news report is objective and uses a headline and lead).", pts: ["Message: key facts - event, date, place, participants, amount raised and the charity supported.", "Message: highlights and quotations from organisers, participants or the charity.", "Message: thanks to helpers and information about future events.", "News report conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph answering the Ws, objective third-person style, quotations.", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, photos referred to, comments invited.", "Newsletter email conventions: subject line, greeting to parents, headings, sign-off from the organisers.", "Language: past tenses, reported speech and precise figures used accurately."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+2+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>Every night this winter, 40 children in our city will sleep in a car.</b></p><p>Not a house. Not a flat. A car.</p><p>Shelter Start helps families move from cars and emergency hostels into safe homes. Just 15 euros pays for a warm sleeping bag and a hot meal. 50 euros covers a week of bus fares so that a parent can attend job interviews.</p><p>'My daughter used to ask why we couldn't go home,' says Mira, a mother we helped last year. 'Now she has her own bedroom.'</p><p>Please give today. Because every child deserves to do their homework somewhere warm.</p><p><i>Shelter Start is a fictional charity.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) What is the main purpose of the text?</p><p>(b) Identify the device used in 'Not a house. Not a flat. A car.' and explain its effect.</p><p>(c) Why does the writer include specific amounts such as 15 euros and 50 euros?</p><p>(d) Which persuasive appeal does Mira's quotation mainly use?</p><p>(e) Identify the call to action.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) to persuade readers to donate money to the charity [1]", "(b) short sentences / repetition / sentence fragments [1]", "(b) emphasises the shocking situation and makes it vivid for the reader [1]", "(c) to show exactly what a donation achieves, making giving feel concrete [1]", "(d) pathos / emotional appeal [1]", "(e) 'Please give today' [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>'We will not wait. We will not whisper. We will not be ignored.'</p></blockquote><p>The repetition of 'We will not' at the start of each sentence in this speech is an example of:</p>", options: ["anaphora", "simile", "onomatopoeia", "understatement"], answer: 0, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: A (anaphora) - Anaphora is the repetition of the same words at the start of successive clauses or sentences."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Councillors, I am here today to ask for a place. Right now, the young people of this town have two choices on a Friday night: the bus shelter or the car park outside the supermarket. Is that really the best we can offer them? A skatepark would cost less than resurfacing one road, and it would give hundreds of teenagers somewhere safe, visible and their own. Give us a place, and we will look after it.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Who is the audience of this speech?</p><p>(b) Identify the rhetorical question in the speech.</p><p>(c) Which comparison does the speaker use to suggest that a skatepark is affordable?</p><p>(d) Identify the list of three (tricolon) in the speech.</p><p>(e) What does the speaker promise in the final sentence?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) the town councillors [1]", "(b) 'Is that really the best we can offer them?' [1]", "(c) a skatepark would cost less than resurfacing one road [1]", "(d) 'safe, visible and their own' [1]", "(e) young people will look after the skatepark [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school is choosing one charity to support this year, and students will vote. Write a text persuading other students to vote for the charity you prefer. Choose one text type: speech at a school assembly, leaflet, or social media post. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear choice of charity with persuasive, developed reasons (its work, local impact, how students can help) and effective rhetoric; high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a register suited to peers.", pts: ["Message: introduces the charity and the problem it addresses, with concrete facts or examples.", "Message: explains why it deserves the vote - impact of donations, local connection, opportunities for students to volunteer.", "Message: responds to likely objections and ends with a clear call to vote.", "Speech conventions: greeting, rhetorical questions, anaphora or tricolon, direct address, closing appeal.", "Leaflet conventions: title and slogan, headings, bullet points, facts, call to action and voting details.", "Social media post conventions: eye-catching opening, hashtags, short paragraphs, tagging and sharing request.", "Language: persuasive devices (pathos, logos, ethos) and accurate, varied structures."] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Few young people in your town know basic first aid. Write a text persuading teenagers to take a free first-aid course offered by a local organisation. Choose one text type: leaflet, speech on local radio, or blog post. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs persuasive reasons (saving lives, confidence, useful for jobs) supported by examples and practical course details; high C needs the chosen type's conventions and an engaging register for teenagers.", pts: ["Message: why first aid matters - real-life situations such as choking, burns, sports injuries, cardiac arrest.", "Message: benefits for teenagers - confidence, certificates for CVs, helping family and friends.", "Message: practical details of the course (dates, place, length, cost: free) and a call to sign up.", "Leaflet conventions: title, slogan, headings, bullet points, contact details.", "Radio speech conventions: greeting listeners, short sentences, repetition, memorable closing line.", "Blog conventions: title, personal anecdote, direct address, link to sign up, comments invited.", "Language: imperatives, conditionals (if someone collapsed...) and persuasive vocabulary used accurately."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "2+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><i>[Intro music fades]</i></p><p><b>JESS:</b> Hey everyone, and welcome back to <i>Lunch Break Science</i>, the podcast where we answer your weirdest questions in under ten minutes. I'm Jess -</p><p><b>MO:</b> - and I'm Mo, and today's question comes from Aiko in Osaka, who wants to know: why does music give you goosebumps?</p><p><b>JESS:</b> Great question, Aiko. So, Mo, have you ever had goosebumps from a song?</p><p><b>MO:</b> Every single time I hear my old school anthem. Please don't laugh.</p><p><i>The podcast and presenters are fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify two features that show this is a podcast script.</p><p>(b) How long are the episodes of the podcast?</p><p>(c) Who sent in today's question?</p><p>(d) What is today's question?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) sound cues, e.g. '[Intro music fades]' [1]", "(a) speakers' names before their lines [1]", "(a) greeting listeners, 'welcome back' [1]", "(a) the show and presenters are introduced by name [1]", "(a) a listener's question / interaction with the audience [1]", "(b) under ten minutes [1]", "(c) Aiko from Osaka [1]", "(d) why music gives you goosebumps [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "A campaign group wants young people to share its message quickly on social media. Which feature is most important for its post?", options: ["A long formal introduction", "A short, eye-catching message with a hashtag and a link", "Footnotes and a bibliography", "The closing formula 'Yours faithfully'"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (A short, eye-catching message with a hashtag and a link) - Social media posts rely on brevity, visual impact, hashtags and links to encourage sharing."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>You won't BELIEVE what this teacher did next!</p></blockquote><p>Headlines like this one are designed mainly to:</p>", options: ["summarise the article accurately", "attract clicks by creating curiosity", "identify the author of the article", "provide sources for the information"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (attract clicks by creating curiosity) - This is clickbait: capital letters and a promise of something surprising make readers curious enough to click."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>★★☆☆☆ 'Looks great, lasts a week'</b></p><p>I really wanted to love these wireless earbuds. The sound is crisp, the case is tiny and they connected to my phone in seconds. But after eight days the left earbud stopped charging, and customer service took eleven days to reply to my email. They offered a replacement, which is fair, but I've lost confidence. Save your money for a sturdier pair.</p><p><i>Verified purchase - posted by Tomás R.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) How many stars did the reviewer give?</p><p>(b) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The reviewer was disappointed by the sound quality.'</p><p>(c) How long did customer service take to reply?</p><p>(d) Which phrase shows that the reviewer thinks the company's response was reasonable?</p><p>(e) What does the reviewer recommend?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) 2 / two stars [1]", "(b) False - 'The sound is crisp' [1]", "(c) eleven days [1]", "(d) 'which is fair' [1]", "(e) save your money for a sturdier pair [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Many students at your school share news stories online without checking whether they are true. Write a text helping students to recognise and avoid misinformation. Choose one text type: blog post, social media post series, or set of guidelines for the school website. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs clear, practical strategies explained with examples (checking sources, reverse image search, looking for other reports, noticing emotional language); high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a register suited to students.", pts: ["Message: explains what misinformation is and why it spreads (shock, emotion, speed of sharing).", "Message: practical checks - source, date, author, other reliable reports, reverse image search, fact-checking sites.", "Message: encourages responsible sharing with a clear call to action.", "Blog conventions: title, personal anecdote, direct address, questions, invitation to comment.", "Social media series conventions: short numbered posts, hashtags, visuals referred to, call to share.", "Guidelines conventions: title, headings, numbered steps, imperatives, summary box.", "Language: imperatives and conditionals (if a headline seems shocking, ...) and digital vocabulary used accurately."] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Hurtful comments have recently appeared on your school's online student forum. Write a text encouraging students to behave respectfully online. Choose one text type: speech at a school assembly, blog post, or set of guidelines. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs developed reasons (effects of cyberbullying, permanence of posts, empathy) and practical advice (think before posting, report, support victims); high C needs the conventions of the chosen type and a register suited to fellow students.", pts: ["Message: describes the problem without naming individuals, and its effects on victims and the school community.", "Message: reasons to behave respectfully - posts are permanent, screenshots, empathy, school rules and the law.", "Message: practical advice - think before posting, block and report, support targets, speak to a trusted adult.", "Speech conventions: greeting, rhetorical questions, direct address, signposting, closing appeal.", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, examples, comments invited.", "Guidelines conventions: title, headings, numbered points, imperatives.", "Language: modal verbs, conditionals and vocabulary of online behaviour (harassment, report, empathy) used accurately."] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-8": {
    units: { a: "Task analysis & planning", b: "Criteria A, B & C", c: "Organisation & cohesion" },
    paper: "P1",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+2", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "<p><b>Task:</b> Your town council is deciding whether to turn the old railway station into a shopping centre or a youth arts centre. Write a text to persuade the council to choose the youth arts centre. Choose one text type: formal letter, speech at a public meeting, or proposal.</p><p>(a) Identify the audience of this task.</p><p>(b) Identify the purpose of the text.</p><p>(c) State which text type you would choose and justify your choice.</p>", rule: "[1] audience, [1] purpose, [1] choice of text type + [1] justification linked to audience, context or purpose. Accept any of the three types if the justification is valid.", pts: ["(a) the town council / the councillors [1]", "(b) to persuade the council to choose the youth arts centre [1]", "(c) a proposal / formal letter / speech is chosen [1]", "(c) justification: it suits a formal adult audience of decision-makers, e.g. a proposal allows headings, costs and recommendations [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your town council is deciding whether to turn the old railway station into a shopping centre or a youth arts centre. Write a text to persuade the council to choose the youth arts centre. Choose one text type: formal letter, speech at a public meeting, or proposal. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clear, persuasive case for the arts centre with developed benefits (creativity, safe space, skills, tourism, local identity) and a response to the shopping-centre option; high C needs a formal register suited to councillors and the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: states the recommendation clearly and early.", "Message: developed benefits - safe space for teenagers, creative skills, jobs and events, preserving the historic building.", "Message: responds to the shopping-centre argument (empty shops, online shopping) and addresses cost and funding.", "Letter conventions: addresses, date, 'Dear Councillors', formal close with full name.", "Speech conventions: greeting the council and public, signposting, rhetorical questions, closing appeal and thanks.", "Proposal conventions: title, To/From/Date/Subject, headings (Background, Proposal, Benefits, Costs), recommendation.", "Language: formal persuasive register, conditionals and accurate complex sentences."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "<p><b>Task:</b> Write a text for your school's website welcoming new international students and giving them practical advice for their first weeks.</p><p>Which text type would be <b>least</b> suitable for this task?</p>", options: ["Blog post", "Set of guidelines", "Diary entry", "Leaflet"], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (Diary entry) - A diary entry is private and not addressed to readers, so it cannot welcome or advise new students."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "3", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "A candidate has 90 minutes for Paper 1. Suggest how the time could be divided between choosing the task, planning, writing and checking.", rule: "[1] per reasonable time allocation with its purpose, maximum [3]. Accept other sensible divisions that total 90 minutes.", pts: ["about 5-10 minutes reading the three tasks and choosing one [1]", "about 10-15 minutes planning ideas, structure and text type conventions [1]", "about 55-60 minutes writing the response [1]", "about 10 minutes checking accuracy, conventions and word count [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "A popular international magazine for young people is publishing a special issue on 'The place I call home'. Write a text describing the town, city or village where you live and what it means to you. Choose one text type: magazine article, blog post, or personal essay. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs vivid, specific description combined with reflection on belonging; high C needs the chosen type's conventions and a register suited to an international readership of young people (cultural references explained).", pts: ["Message: specific description of places, sounds, food and people that make the place distinctive.", "Message: reflection on what home means - belonging, memories, identity - beyond a tourist description.", "Message: explains local references for international readers.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, engaging lead, subheadings.", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, photos referred to, direct address and comments invited.", "Personal essay conventions: title, reflective first person, developed paragraphs, thoughtful conclusion.", "Language: descriptive vocabulary, imagery and varied sentence structures used accurately."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><i>Extract from a candidate's travel blog:</i> Dear Sir or Madam, I am writing this blog post to inform you about my holiday in Kyoto. Firstly, the temples were breathtaking, especially Kinkaku-ji, whose golden walls shimmered in the afternoon light. Secondly, the food was exquisite. In conclusion, I recommend Kyoto.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify one strength of the extract relevant to Criterion A (Language).</p><p>(b) Identify one weakness of the extract relevant to Criterion C (Conceptual understanding).</p><p>(c) Suggest one change that would improve the extract for Criterion C.</p>", rule: "[1] per relevant point for each part. Accept any valid answer linked to the named criterion.", pts: ["(a) precise, varied vocabulary such as 'breathtaking', 'shimmered', 'exquisite' [1]", "(a) a complex sentence with a relative clause ('whose golden walls shimmered') [1]", "(b) the formal letter greeting 'Dear Sir or Madam' is inappropriate for a blog / the register is too formal for blog readers [1]", "(b) there is no blog title or engaging opening [1]", "(c) add a catchy title and an informal greeting to readers, e.g. 'Hi fellow travellers!' [1]", "(c) use a personal, conversational tone with anecdotes and an invitation to comment [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "A response follows the conventions of a speech perfectly and uses an appropriate register, but its ideas are repetitive and undeveloped. Which criterion is most affected?", options: ["Criterion A: Language", "Criterion B: Message", "Criterion C: Conceptual understanding", "All three criteria equally"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (Criterion B: Message) - Criterion B rewards relevant, developed and well-organised ideas; conventions and register belong to Criterion C."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "2+2", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "Explain the difference between Criterion B (Message) and Criterion C (Conceptual understanding) in Paper 1, giving an example of what each rewards.", rule: "[1] for what each criterion assesses + [1] for a relevant example, twice.", pts: ["Criterion B rewards relevant ideas that are developed and organised [1]; e.g. giving specific reasons and examples in a persuasive article, linked with cohesive devices [1]", "Criterion C rewards the choice of text type, register, tone and conventions [1]; e.g. using a headline, byline and subheadings in an article, or a formal register when writing to a principal [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><i>Conclusion of a candidate's proposal for a school garden:</i> In conclusion, the garden is a good idea. It is good for the environment and students will like it. Thank you.</p></blockquote><p>Suggest two ways in which this conclusion could be improved for Criterion B (Message).</p>", rule: "[1] per valid suggestion, maximum [2].", pts: ["summarise the main proposals with specific benefits (e.g. fresh food for the canteen, outdoor science lessons) [1]", "make a clear recommendation or request for approval, e.g. that the project start in spring [1]", "address the reader's concerns such as cost and maintenance [1]", "use cohesive devices and more developed sentences to link the ideas [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your city is hosting an international youth sports tournament next year, and residents are divided about the cost. Write a text giving your view on whether the city should host the event. Choose one text type: opinion column, letter to the mayor, or speech at a town hall meeting. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High A needs varied, accurate vocabulary and complex structures; high B needs a clear, balanced and developed argument (costs, tourism, facilities, disruption, legacy); high C needs the conventions and register of the chosen type.", pts: ["Message: clear position on hosting the tournament, stated early and restated at the end.", "Message: arguments - tourism and income, new facilities, inspiration for young people versus cost, traffic and disruption.", "Message: concession and rebuttal; practical suggestions (use existing venues, volunteers, legacy plan).", "Opinion column conventions: headline, byline, provocative opening, rhetorical questions.", "Letter conventions: 'Dear Mayor', formal tone, clear purpose, formal close with full name.", "Speech conventions: greeting the audience, signposting, rhetorical devices, appeal and thanks.", "Language: varied complex structures and topic vocabulary (legacy, infrastructure, investment) used accurately."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>A. As a result, many villages lost their only shop.<br>B. In the 1990s, large supermarkets began opening on the edges of towns.<br>C. However, a new wave of community-owned shops is now reversing this trend.<br>D. Shoppers, attracted by lower prices, started driving out of town to do their weekly shop.</p></blockquote><p>The sentences of a paragraph have been jumbled. Which is the most logical order?</p>", options: ["B, D, A, C", "D, B, C, A", "B, A, D, C", "C, B, D, A"], answer: 0, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: A (B, D, A, C) - B sets the scene in time; D explains the effect on shoppers; 'As a result' (A) gives the consequence; 'However' (C) introduces the contrasting recent change."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "3", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Many people believe that homework improves results. However, recent studies suggest that the benefits are small for younger pupils. Moreover, too much homework can cause stress. Consequently, some schools have replaced it with reading for pleasure.</p></blockquote><p>Identify three cohesive devices in this paragraph and state the function of each.</p>", rule: "[1] per device correctly identified with its function, maximum [3].", pts: ["'However' introduces a contrast [1]", "'Moreover' adds a further point [1]", "'Consequently' introduces a result [1]", "'it' refers back to homework (reference) [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Electric scooters have become popular in many cities. (a) ______ being cheap and convenient, they are also involved in a growing number of accidents. Some cities, (b) ______ Paris, have banned rental scooters altogether, (c) ______ others have introduced speed limits and parking zones.</p></blockquote><p>Complete gaps (a)-(c) with the most appropriate word or phrase from the list. There are more words than gaps.</p><p>despite &nbsp; therefore &nbsp; for instance &nbsp; whereas &nbsp; unless</p>", rule: "[1] per correct gap. Only the words from the list are accepted.", pts: ["(a) Despite [1]", "(b) for instance [1]", "(c) whereas [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1", html: true, t: "Which sentence would be the most effective topic sentence for a paragraph about the benefits of cycling to school?", options: ["Cycling to school brings clear benefits for students' health, concentration and independence.", "I have a bike and I like it.", "There are many things to say about this topic.", "Also, cars are bad, and buses too."], answer: 0, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: A (Cycling to school brings clear benefits for students' health, concentration and independence.) - A topic sentence introduces the paragraph's main idea clearly and signals the points to follow."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P1", t: "A report on a school recycling scheme has three sections: (1) why the scheme was set up and how data were collected; (2) what the data showed; (3) what should be done next. Suggest a suitable heading for each section.", rule: "[1] per appropriate report heading.", pts: ["(1) Introduction / Purpose and method / Background [1]", "(2) Findings / Results [1]", "(3) Recommendations / Next steps [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school is considering replacing written end-of-year exams with project-based assessment. Write a text giving your views on the proposal. Choose one text type: article for the school magazine, speech for a school debate, or formal email to the head of studies. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High B needs a clearly organised argument: an introduction stating the position, logically sequenced paragraphs with topic sentences, effective cohesive devices and a conclusion; high C needs the chosen type's conventions and an appropriate register.", pts: ["Organisation: introduction stating the issue and position; one main idea per paragraph with a topic sentence; conclusion with a recommendation.", "Message: arguments for projects - deeper learning, real-world skills, less exam stress.", "Message: arguments against - fairness, plagiarism and AI use, workload; balanced judgement.", "Cohesion: varied linking devices for addition, contrast and consequence (furthermore, on the other hand, consequently).", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead, subheadings.", "Speech or email conventions: greeting, signposting or subject line, appropriate closing.", "Language: accurate complex sentences and academic vocabulary (assessment, collaboration, criteria)."] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-9": {
    units: { a: "Reading strategies", b: "Question types", c: "Listening comprehension" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>In defence of boredom</b><p><b>[1]</b> When did you last do nothing? No scrolling, no streaming, no 'catching up' on messages - simply sitting and letting your mind wander? If you cannot remember, you are in good company. Surveys suggest that many of us reach for a phone within seconds of feeling even slightly bored.</p><p><b>[2]</b> This is a pity, because boredom may be useful. In one frequently cited experiment, volunteers who first completed a dull task - copying numbers from a telephone directory - later came up with more creative uses for a pair of plastic cups than volunteers who had skipped the dull task. The researchers suggested that a bored mind goes looking for stimulation, and sometimes finds ideas.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Boredom is hardly always pleasant or productive. For people in repetitive jobs, or teenagers with nowhere to go, chronic boredom can lead to frustration and risky behaviour. The point is to stop treating every empty minute as an emergency, rather than to celebrate dullness.</p><p><b>[4]</b> So here is my modest proposal: leave your phone in another room for twenty minutes a day. Stare out of the window. Let the silence become uncomfortable. You might be surprised by what turns up.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify the text type.</p><p>(b) Which phrase in paragraph 1 shows that the writer believes the habit is common?</p><p>(c) In the experiment, what did the volunteers do first?</p><p>(d) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'The bored volunteers were less creative than the others.'</p><p>(e) Find the word in paragraph 3 that means 'long-lasting or constant'.</p><p>(f) What does the writer suggest readers should do?</p><p>(g) Which phrase in paragraph 4 shows that the writer's suggestion is presented as small and reasonable?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) opinion column / opinion article / blog post [1]", "(b) 'you are in good company' [1]", "(c) copied numbers from a telephone directory / completed a dull task [1]", "(d) False - they 'came up with more creative uses for a pair of plastic cups' [1]", "(e) chronic [1]", "(f) leave their phone in another room for twenty minutes a day [1]", "(g) 'my modest proposal' [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Libraries are reinventing themselves. Alongside books, many now lend laptops, run coding clubs, host job-search workshops and offer a warm, free space to anyone who walks in. For some visitors, the library is the only place in their day where nobody expects them to buy anything.</p></blockquote><p>What is the main idea of this text?</p>", options: ["Libraries are closing because nobody reads books.", "Libraries are expanding their role far beyond lending books.", "Libraries have started charging visitors for entry.", "Libraries now lend only laptops."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (Libraries are expanding their role far beyond lending books.) - Skimming the topic sentence ('reinventing themselves') and the list of new services shows the main idea."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p><b>KEEP OUR BEACHES BEAUTIFUL!</b><br>Join the Big Beach Clean - Saturday 6 May, 10:00-13:00, North Bay car park.<br>Gloves, bags and snacks provided.<br>Bring a friend, bring your family - every bottle counts!<br><i>Under-14s must be accompanied by an adult.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify the text type.</p><p>(b) What is the purpose of the text?</p><p>(c) Identify one language feature typical of this text type.</p><p>(d) What must children under fourteen do?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) leaflet / flyer / poster [1]", "(b) to persuade people to take part in the beach clean [1]", "(c) imperative verbs such as 'Join' or 'Bring' [1]", "(c) capitalised slogan or headline with an exclamation mark [1]", "(c) brief practical details: date, time and place [1]", "(d) be accompanied by an adult [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Grandma never said she was lonely. But every Sunday, when we got up to leave, she found another photo album she simply had to show us, and the kettle always needed boiling one more time.</p></blockquote><p>What can be inferred from this text?</p>", options: ["Grandma dislikes having visitors.", "Grandma wants the family to stay longer.", "Grandma's kettle is broken.", "Grandma has just bought some new photo albums."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (Grandma wants the family to stay longer.) - The text never says so directly, but finding 'another photo album' and boiling the kettle 'one more time' are ways of delaying the family's departure."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The new manager arrived at 7 a.m. on her first day, an hour before anyone else. By lunchtime she had learned the names of all thirty staff, including the night cleaners, whom her predecessor had ignored for years.</p></blockquote><p>(a) At what time did the new manager arrive on her first day?</p><p>(b) What does the text imply about the previous manager?</p><p>(c) What does the text suggest about the new manager's attitude to her staff?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) 7 a.m. [1]", "(b) he or she ignored some staff such as the night cleaners / showed little respect for them [1]", "(c) she is respectful and interested in all her staff / values everyone equally [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Valmont's car-free Sundays</b><p><b>[1]</b> On the first Sunday of every month, the streets of Valmont fall quiet. From 8 a.m. to 6 p.m., private cars are banned from the town centre, and the roads belong to cyclists, roller-skaters, street musicians and families with pushchairs.</p><p><b>[2]</b> The idea came from a group of secondary-school students who measured air quality outside their school as a science project. Their readings, taken over six months, showed pollution levels that regularly exceeded the national limit during the morning rush hour. They presented their results to the town council, which agreed to a one-day trial.</p><p><b>[3]</b> Shopkeepers were initially hostile, fearing they would lose customers. To their surprise, sales on car-free Sundays have risen, as people linger in cafés and browse rather than drive to an out-of-town shopping centre. Some business owners now complain that the scheme happens too rarely.</p><p><b>[4]</b> The students who started it have since left school, but they remain proud. 'We were told that young people can't change anything,' says one of them, now studying environmental engineering. 'We just wanted to breathe.'</p><p><i>Valmont is a fictional town.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Between which times are cars banned on car-free Sundays?</p><p>(b) Who first came up with the idea?</p><p>(c) For how long did they take air-quality readings?</p><p>(d) Find the word in paragraph 2 that means 'went beyond'.</p><p>(e) The following statement is either true or false. Choose the correct option, then justify your answer with a relevant brief quotation from the text: 'Shopkeepers supported the scheme from the start.'</p><p>(f) Complete the sentence with words from paragraph 3: On car-free Sundays, people spend time in cafés instead of driving to ...</p><p>(g) In 'the scheme happens too rarely' (paragraph 3), what does 'the scheme' refer to?</p><p>(h) What does the final remark 'We just wanted to breathe' suggest about the students' motivation?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) 8 a.m. and 6 p.m. [1]", "(b) a group of secondary-school students [1]", "(c) six months [1]", "(d) exceeded [1]", "(e) False - 'Shopkeepers were initially hostile' [1]", "(f) an out-of-town shopping centre [1]", "(g) car-free Sundays / the monthly ban on cars in the town centre [1]", "(h) their aim was simply cleaner air and better health [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The Pomodoro Technique, developed by Francesco Cirillo in the late 1980s, breaks work into 25-minute periods separated by short breaks. After four such periods, the worker takes a longer break. The method is named after the tomato-shaped kitchen timer that Cirillo used as a university student - <i>pomodoro</i> is the Italian word for tomato.</p></blockquote><p>Complete each sentence beginning (a)-(c) with the correct ending (A-E). Write the letter and the full ending.</p><p>(a) The technique divides work into ...</p><p>(b) After four work periods ...</p><p>(c) The name of the technique comes from ...</p><p>A. a longer break is taken.<br>B. 25-minute periods with short breaks between them.<br>C. a kitchen timer shaped like a tomato.<br>D. one long session without any breaks.<br>E. Cirillo's favourite food.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) B - 25-minute periods with short breaks between them [1]", "(b) A - a longer break is taken [1]", "(c) C - a kitchen timer shaped like a tomato [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>After months of rumours, the company finally confirmed yesterday that its flagship store would close in January. Staff, many of whom had worked there for decades, were informed by email - a decision one employee described as 'cowardly'. A spokesperson insisted that every worker would be offered a role elsewhere in the business.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Find the word in the text that means 'most important or leading' (of a company's shops).</p><p>(b) Find the word that shows strong criticism of the way staff were told.</p><p>(c) Which phrase shows that the closure came as little surprise?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) flagship [1]", "(b) cowardly [1]", "(c) 'After months of rumours' [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>After months of rumours, the company finally confirmed yesterday that its flagship store would close in January. Staff, many of whom had worked there for decades, were informed by email - a decision one employee described as 'cowardly'. A spokesperson insisted that every worker would be offered a role elsewhere in the business.</p></blockquote><p>In 'many of <b>whom</b> had worked there for decades', 'whom' refers to:</p>", options: ["the company", "the staff", "the spokesperson", "the customers"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (the staff) - 'Whom' follows 'Staff' and refers back to the employees."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The museum, (a) ______ collection includes more than 10,000 objects, attracts visitors from all over the world. (b) ______ its fame, it receives very little government funding. It (c) ______ relies heavily on donations from visitors.</p><p><i>The museum is fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>Complete gaps (a)-(c) with the most appropriate word from the list. There are more words than gaps.</p><p>although &nbsp; therefore &nbsp; despite &nbsp; whose &nbsp; unless</p>", rule: "[1] per correct gap. Only words from the list are accepted.", pts: ["(a) whose [1]", "(b) Despite [1]", "(c) therefore [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (radio interview)</i><p><b>Presenter:</b> Lucas, you keep bees on the roof of a hotel. How did that start?</p><p><b>Lucas:</b> By accident, really. The hotel manager wanted a green project for the roof, and my uncle, who's kept bees for forty years, suggested hives. I was fifteen and bored that summer, so I volunteered to help him.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> How many hives are there now?</p><p><b>Lucas:</b> Six, with roughly fifty thousand bees in each one in midsummer.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> Isn't the city a bad place for bees?</p><p><b>Lucas:</b> People assume so, but city parks and gardens have a huge variety of flowers, and often fewer pesticides than farms. Our honey tastes different every month.</p><p><b>Presenter:</b> And the hotel guests?</p><p><b>Lucas:</b> The honey is served at breakfast. Some guests ask to visit the roof - in a protective suit, of course.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Whose idea was it to put hives on the roof?</p><p>(b) Why did Lucas volunteer to help?</p><p>(c) How many hives are there now?</p><p>(d) Give one reason why the city is a good place for bees.</p><p>(e) Where is the honey served?</p><p>(f) What must guests wear to visit the roof?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) his uncle's [1]", "(b) he was bored that summer [1]", "(c) 6 / six hives [1]", "(d) parks and gardens have a huge variety of flowers [1]", "(d) cities often use fewer pesticides than farms [1]", "(e) at breakfast in the hotel [1]", "(f) a protective suit [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (station announcement)</i><p><b>Announcer:</b> Attention, please. The 14:20 service to Northfield is delayed by approximately 25 minutes because of a signal failure. This train will now depart from platform 6, instead of platform 2. Passengers for Eastbridge should change at Kenton. Refreshments are available at the café on platform 1. We apologise for the inconvenience.</p></blockquote><p>(a) Why is the train delayed?</p><p>(b) From which platform will the train now depart?</p><p>(c) Where should passengers for Eastbridge change trains?</p><p>(d) Where can passengers buy refreshments?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) a signal failure [1]", "(b) platform 6 / six [1]", "(c) Kenton [1]", "(d) the café on platform 1 [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (podcast)</i><p><b>Speaker:</b> Look, I'm all for tourism - I run a guesthouse! But when the town has more cruise passengers than residents on a Tuesday afternoon, something has gone wrong. We need limits, not bans.</p></blockquote><p>What is the speaker's attitude to tourism?</p>", options: ["Completely opposed to it", "In favour of unlimited growth", "Supportive, but wants it controlled", "Indifferent to it"], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (Supportive, but wants it controlled) - The speaker benefits from tourism ('I run a guesthouse') but wants 'limits, not bans'."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (voicemail message)</i><p><b>Voicemail:</b> Hi, this is Maria from Greenway Dental Clinic, calling for Mr James Owusu. I'm afraid Dr Patel is ill tomorrow, so we need to move your appointment from Thursday at ten to Friday at half past three. If that time is a problem, please call us back before five o'clock today. Also, remember to bring your new insurance card. Thanks, bye!</p></blockquote><p>Complete the notes.</p><p>(a) New appointment: ...</p><p>(b) Reason for the change: ...</p><p>(c) Call the clinic back before: ...</p><p>(d) Remember to bring: ...</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) Friday at half past three / 3:30 [1]", "(b) Dr Patel is ill [1]", "(c) five o'clock today [1]", "(d) new insurance card [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (student discussion)</i><p><b>Amir:</b> Uniforms save money in the long run. Parents don't have to buy fashionable clothes for every day of the week.</p><p><b>Bea:</b> I disagree. Uniforms are expensive when you can only buy them from one shop.</p><p><b>Chen:</b> For me the issue is identity, more than cost. I'd like to be able to express who I am.</p></blockquote><p>Which speaker (Amir, Bea or Chen)...</p><p>(a) believes uniforms reduce costs for families?</p><p>(b) is mainly concerned about self-expression?</p><p>(c) complains that there is only one supplier?</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) Amir [1]", "(b) Chen [1]", "(c) Bea [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><i>Audio transcript (award ceremony)</i><p><b>Presenter:</b> And the winner of this year's Young Writer award is... Tilly Okonjo!</p><p><b>Tilly:</b> Oh my goodness. I - I really didn't expect this. I haven't even prepared a speech!</p></blockquote><p>How does Tilly react to the announcement?</p>", options: ["She is indifferent.", "She is surprised.", "She is annoyed.", "She is disappointed."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (She is surprised.) - Her hesitation ('I - I'), 'Oh my goodness' and 'didn't expect this' show surprise."] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-10": {
    units: { a: "Format & criteria", b: "Presenting the extract", c: "Discussion & interaction" },
    paper: "IO",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "In the individual oral, the candidate's 3-4 minute presentation on the literary extract is immediately followed by:", options: ["a general conversation on a different theme", "a discussion with the teacher about the extract", "a short written reflection", "a second presentation on another extract"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (a discussion with the teacher about the extract) - The order is: presentation on the extract (3-4 min), follow-up discussion on the extract (4-5 min), then general conversation on at least one additional theme (5-6 min)."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "IO", t: "A candidate gives a well-prepared presentation on the extract but answers the teacher's follow-up questions with only one or two words. Explain which criterion is most affected and why.", rule: "[1] for identifying the criterion + [1] for a valid explanation.", pts: ["Criterion C (Interactive skills) is most affected [1]", "the answers are not sustained or developed and show no initiative in the conversation [1]", "B1 also suffers because observations about the extract are not developed in the discussion [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "IO", t: "In the general conversation, a candidate talks only about the theme already covered by the extract. Explain why this is a problem.", rule: "[1] requirement + [1] consequence.", pts: ["the general conversation must cover at least one additional theme from the course [1]", "so the candidate limits what can be credited under B2 (Message: conversation) / the requirement of the task is not met [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "IO", t: "Outline two features of spoken language that are rewarded under Criterion A (Language) in the individual oral.", rule: "[1] per valid feature, maximum [2].", pts: ["varied and idiomatic vocabulary [1]", "a range of complex grammatical structures [1]", "accuracy, with errors that do not interfere with communication [1]", "clear pronunciation and intonation [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Extract A</b><p>The letter had been lying on the hall table for three days before Nadia opened it. She knew the handwriting - the careful, looping script of a man who had learned to write with a fountain pen and never forgiven the world for inventing ballpoints. She walked past it in the mornings and again in the evenings, and each time the envelope seemed a little larger.</p><p>On the fourth day she sat on the bottom stair and slid a knife under the flap. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, and a photograph of a boy she did not recognise standing in front of a house she did. On the back of the photograph, in the same looping hand, were four words: 'He should know you.'</p><p>Nadia read the words until they stopped meaning anything. Then she turned the photograph face down on her knee, as if the boy might be watching her.</p><p><i>Original extract written for practice.</i></p></blockquote><p>Prepare a 3-4 minute presentation on Extract A, linking it to one of the course themes, and be ready to discuss it with your teacher.</p>", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B1 5-6 needs a focused presentation that refers closely to details of the extract and develops an interpretation; C 5-6 needs confident, sustained responses in the follow-up discussion.", pts: ["Context and summary: Nadia receives a letter from an older man she knows and avoids opening it for three days.", "Detail: 'each time the envelope seemed a little larger' shows growing anxiety; the handwriting suggests an old-fashioned, unforgiving character.", "Interpretation: the photograph and 'He should know you' suggest a hidden family connection, perhaps a child Nadia has never met.", "Final image: turning the photograph face down shows guilt or fear of facing the past.", "Link to theme: Identities (family relationships, belonging) or Experiences (life stories, the past returning).", "Language: accurate, varied vocabulary for discussing literature (suggests, conveys, symbolises, tension)."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "2+1+2+2", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Extract A</b><p>The letter had been lying on the hall table for three days before Nadia opened it. She knew the handwriting - the careful, looping script of a man who had learned to write with a fountain pen and never forgiven the world for inventing ballpoints. She walked past it in the mornings and again in the evenings, and each time the envelope seemed a little larger.</p><p>On the fourth day she sat on the bottom stair and slid a knife under the flap. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, and a photograph of a boy she did not recognise standing in front of a house she did. On the back of the photograph, in the same looping hand, were four words: 'He should know you.'</p><p>Nadia read the words until they stopped meaning anything. Then she turned the photograph face down on her knee, as if the boy might be watching her.</p><p><i>Original extract written for practice.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify two details from the extract that you could quote to show Nadia's reluctance to open the letter.</p><p>(b) What does the description of the handwriting suggest about the person who wrote the letter?</p><p>(c) Explain the effect of the final sentence of the extract.</p><p>(d) Suggest one course theme to which you could link the extract, and explain the link.</p>", rule: "[1] per valid point. Quotations must come from the extract; interpretations must be supported by the text.", pts: ["(a) the letter lay on the hall table for three days before she opened it [1]", "(a) she walked past it in the mornings and again in the evenings [1]", "(a) 'each time the envelope seemed a little larger' [1]", "(b) the writer is old-fashioned / careful / resentful of change, someone who 'never forgave' the modern world [1]", "(c) it shows Nadia's guilt or discomfort [1]", "(c) turning the photograph face down suggests she cannot yet face the boy or her past [1]", "(d) Identities: family relationships and belonging [1]; the unknown boy may be a relative Nadia has never met [1]", "(d) Experiences: life stories [1]; a past event or relationship suddenly returns into Nadia's life [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "2+1+1+2", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Extract B</b><p>Grandpa Teo held the tablet the way other people hold a wet fish. 'And where,' he asked, 'is the button?'</p><p>'There isn't one. You just touch the screen.'</p><p>He touched it, gently, then harder, then with a kind of fury, as if the glass had insulted his mother. The screen woke up and showed him a photograph of his granddaughter in Toronto, laughing at something he could not hear.</p><p>'She's there?' he whispered. 'Now?'</p><p>'If you press the green circle, she'll answer.'</p><p>He sat for a long time looking at the photograph, his thumb hovering above the green circle like a bird that could not decide whether to land. Finally he handed the tablet back. 'Tomorrow,' he said. 'I want to put on a clean shirt first.'</p><p><i>Original extract written for practice.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) Identify the simile used to describe how Teo holds the tablet, and explain its effect.</p><p>(b) Which phrase shows Teo's frustration with the screen?</p><p>(c) What does Teo's final remark, 'I want to put on a clean shirt first', reveal about his feelings?</p><p>(d) Suggest how this extract could be linked to the theme Human ingenuity.</p>", rule: "[1] per valid point. Accept OWTTE for interpretations supported by the extract.", pts: ["(a) 'the way other people hold a wet fish' [1]", "(a) it shows his awkwardness and discomfort with unfamiliar technology [1]", "(b) 'with a kind of fury, as if the glass had insulted his mother' [1]", "(c) he treats the call as an important occasion and wants to look respectable for his granddaughter / he is nervous [1]", "(d) technology connects families separated by distance [1]", "(d) the extract contrasts generations' experience of technology / the digital divide [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+2+1", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Extract C</b><p>At the airport the woman at the desk asked Amina to spell her name, and then to spell it again. Amina said each letter clearly, the way her English teacher back home had taught her, but the woman typed something else anyway. When the card came out of the machine it said AMENA.</p><p>'It's fine,' her father said, in the language that suddenly felt very loud. 'It's only a card.'</p><p>But on the bus Amina held the card on her knee and looked at the stranger's name for the whole journey, past grey motorways and fields so green they looked painted. By the time they reached the hostel she had decided two things: that she would learn to speak English so well that nobody would ever misspell her name again, and that she would never, ever answer to Amena.</p><p><i>Original extract written for practice.</i></p></blockquote><p>(a) What mistake is made at the airport desk?</p><p>(b) Which phrase shows that Amina's family feel self-conscious using their own language in public?</p><p>(c) What does the comparison 'fields so green they looked painted' suggest about Amina's impression of the new country?</p><p>(d) Identify the two decisions Amina makes at the end of the extract.</p><p>(e) What do these decisions reveal about Amina's character?</p>", rule: "[1] per valid point. Accept OWTTE for interpretations supported by the extract.", pts: ["(a) her name is misspelled on the card as AMENA [1]", "(b) 'in the language that suddenly felt very loud' [1]", "(c) the country seems unreal / unfamiliar / strikingly different from home [1]", "(d) she will learn English so well that nobody misspells her name again [1]", "(d) she will answer only to her real name, Amina [1]", "(e) she is determined and proud, with a strong sense of her own identity [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Extract B</b><p>Grandpa Teo held the tablet the way other people hold a wet fish. 'And where,' he asked, 'is the button?'</p><p>'There isn't one. You just touch the screen.'</p><p>He touched it, gently, then harder, then with a kind of fury, as if the glass had insulted his mother. The screen woke up and showed him a photograph of his granddaughter in Toronto, laughing at something he could not hear.</p><p>'She's there?' he whispered. 'Now?'</p><p>'If you press the green circle, she'll answer.'</p><p>He sat for a long time looking at the photograph, his thumb hovering above the green circle like a bird that could not decide whether to land. Finally he handed the tablet back. 'Tomorrow,' he said. 'I want to put on a clean shirt first.'</p><p><i>Original extract written for practice.</i></p></blockquote><p>Prepare a 3-4 minute presentation on Extract B, linking it to the theme Human ingenuity or Identities.</p>", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B1 5-6 needs close reference to details of the extract (similes, actions, dialogue) and a developed interpretation linked to a theme.", pts: ["Context: an elderly man is shown how to make a video call to his granddaughter abroad.", "Detail: the similes ('a wet fish', 'like a bird that could not decide whether to land') show awkwardness and hesitation.", "Interpretation: the technology is less important than the emotional weight of the call - love, distance, nervousness.", "Final line: wanting a clean shirt shows respect and how much the contact means to him.", "Link to theme: Human ingenuity (technology and communication across distance) or Identities (ageing, family across countries).", "Language: accurate literary vocabulary and a clear structure (introduction, two or three points, conclusion)."] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Extract C</b><p>At the airport the woman at the desk asked Amina to spell her name, and then to spell it again. Amina said each letter clearly, the way her English teacher back home had taught her, but the woman typed something else anyway. When the card came out of the machine it said AMENA.</p><p>'It's fine,' her father said, in the language that suddenly felt very loud. 'It's only a card.'</p><p>But on the bus Amina held the card on her knee and looked at the stranger's name for the whole journey, past grey motorways and fields so green they looked painted. By the time they reached the hostel she had decided two things: that she would learn to speak English so well that nobody would ever misspell her name again, and that she would never, ever answer to Amena.</p><p><i>Original extract written for practice.</i></p></blockquote><p>Prepare a 3-4 minute presentation on Extract C, linking it to the theme Experiences or Identities.</p>", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B1 5-6 needs specific references to the extract and a developed interpretation of Amina's experience linked to a theme.", pts: ["Context: a girl arrives in a new country with her father and her name is misspelled on an official card.", "Detail: 'the language that suddenly felt very loud' shows self-consciousness; 'the stranger's name' shows that the card feels foreign to her.", "Interpretation: a name stands for identity; the small bureaucratic mistake feels like an attempt to erase who she is.", "Ending: her two decisions show determination to integrate through language while keeping her identity.", "Link to theme: Experiences (migration) or Identities (language and identity, belonging).", "Language: accurate literary vocabulary (symbolises, conveys, contrast) and a clear structure."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "3", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "<blockquote><b>Extract A</b><p>The letter had been lying on the hall table for three days before Nadia opened it. She knew the handwriting - the careful, looping script of a man who had learned to write with a fountain pen and never forgiven the world for inventing ballpoints. She walked past it in the mornings and again in the evenings, and each time the envelope seemed a little larger.</p><p>On the fourth day she sat on the bottom stair and slid a knife under the flap. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, and a photograph of a boy she did not recognise standing in front of a house she did. On the back of the photograph, in the same looping hand, were four words: 'He should know you.'</p><p>Nadia read the words until they stopped meaning anything. Then she turned the photograph face down on her knee, as if the boy might be watching her.</p><p><i>Original extract written for practice.</i></p></blockquote><p>In the follow-up discussion the teacher asks: 'Why do you think the writer never tells us who the boy is?' Outline how a candidate could respond effectively.</p>", rule: "[1] per valid element of an effective response, maximum [3].", pts: ["offer an interpretation, e.g. it creates mystery and suspense, so the reader shares Nadia's uncertainty [1]", "support the interpretation with a detail from the extract, such as the words 'He should know you' [1]", "extend the answer, e.g. by suggesting an alternative reading or linking to a theme such as family identity [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "During the discussion, the teacher asks a question that the candidate does not fully understand. What is the best response?", options: ["Stay silent until the teacher moves on", "'Sorry, could you rephrase the question, please?'", "Recite a prepared answer to a different question", "Change the subject to a favourite theme"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B ('Sorry, could you rephrase the question, please?') - Asking for clarification is a natural interactive strategy and is credited under Criterion C."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "IO", t: "Give two phrases a candidate could use to disagree politely with the teacher in the general conversation.", rule: "[1] per appropriate phrase, maximum [2].", pts: ["'I see your point, but I think...' [1]", "'That's true to some extent; however...' [1]", "'I'm not entirely sure I agree, because...' [1]", "'That's an interesting view, although in my experience...' [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "IO", t: "After a presentation on Extract A, the teacher asks: 'Would you have opened the letter?' Explain why this personal question is an opportunity for the candidate.", rule: "[1] per valid point linked to the criteria.", pts: ["it allows the candidate to show a personal response and natural, spontaneous interaction (Criterion C) [1]", "the candidate can connect personal experience to the extract's ideas and develop the answer further (B1) [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "IO", html: true, t: "In the general conversation the teacher asks: 'Do you think social media brings families closer?' Which answer best demonstrates strong interactive skills?", options: ["'Yes.'", "'I don't know.'", "'In some ways, yes - my grandmother video-calls us every Sunday from Lagos. But I sometimes wonder whether quick messages replace real conversations. What do you think?'", "'Social media is a type of website.'"], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C ('In some ways, yes - my grandmother video-calls us every Sunday from Lagos. But I sometimes wonder whether quick messages replace real conversations. What do you think?') - It gives an opinion, supports it with a personal example, adds nuance and invites the teacher to respond."] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+6+6+6", paper: "IO", t: "General conversation (theme: Social organization). The teacher asks: 'Should schools teach more practical life skills, such as cooking, managing money and first aid?' Outline how you would develop and sustain your answer.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], B1 Message: literary extract [6], B2 Message: conversation [6], C Interactive skills [6] = 30. B2 5-6 needs a clear, developed opinion with examples; C 5-6 needs spontaneous, sustained interaction, including responding to follow-up questions and taking initiative.", pts: ["Clear opinion with reasons: practical skills prepare students for independent life.", "Examples: budgeting and avoiding debt, cooking healthily on a student budget, first aid in emergencies.", "Counter-argument: limited timetable time; families also have a role; academic subjects matter.", "Personal or cultural connection: what the candidate's school or country offers.", "Interaction: asking for the teacher's view, extending answers, asking for clarification."] },
    ],
  },
  "engb-11": {
    units: { a: "Tense & structure accuracy", b: "Vocabulary & idiom", c: "Complex sentences & cohesion" },
    paper: "P1",
    qs: [
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>When I (a) ______ (arrive) in Lisbon last September, I (b) ______ (only / visit) Portugal once before. Since then I (c) ______ (make) friends from twelve countries, and by the end of this academic year I (d) ______ (finish) my first year at university.</p></blockquote><p>Complete gaps (a)-(d) with the correct form of the verb in brackets.</p>", rule: "[1] per correct verb form. Spelling must be accurate.", pts: ["(a) arrived [1]", "(b) had only visited [1]", "(c) have made [1]", "(d) will have finished [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first, beginning with the words given.<br>(a) People say that the castle is haunted. → The castle ...<br>(b) I regret not studying harder for the test. → I wish ...<br>(c) It's possible that she missed the bus. → She ...", rule: "[1] per correct transformation; the meaning must be kept.", pts: ["(a) The castle is said to be haunted [1]", "(b) I wish I had studied harder for the test [1]", "(c) She may have missed the bus / She might have missed the bus [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Choose the option that best completes the sentence: 'If I ______ more free time, I would volunteer at the animal shelter.'", options: ["have", "had", "will have", "would have"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (had) - A hypothetical present situation (second conditional) uses the past simple in the if-clause: 'If I had more free time, I would...'."] },
      { u: "a", s: "A", m: "1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Report the following statement in reported speech: 'We are opening a second café next year,' the owner told me.", rule: "[1] backshift of the verb + [1] change of time reference (and pronoun).", pts: ["The owner told me (that) they were opening a second café - the verb is backshifted to the past [1]", "the time reference changes to 'the following year' / 'the next year' [1]"] },
      { u: "a", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Write a text about a time when you had to make a difficult decision, explaining what happened and what you learned from it. Choose one text type: blog post, personal essay, or diary entry. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High A needs a range of tenses used accurately (past simple, past continuous, past perfect for background, present perfect and present tenses for reflection, conditionals for alternatives); high B needs a clear narrative with genuine reflection; high C needs the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: sets the scene and explains the choice and why it was difficult.", "Message: narrates the decision and its consequences with specific details.", "Message: reflects on what was learned and how the writer has changed.", "Language: past perfect for earlier events (I had already promised...), third conditional for alternatives (If I had refused, ...).", "Blog conventions: title, personal voice, direct address, invitation to comment.", "Personal essay conventions: title, reflective structure, developed paragraphs, thoughtful conclusion.", "Diary conventions: date, intimate first person, private emotions."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>The documentary is a meticulous account of the mountain village's decline. Its director, who spent two years living among the remaining residents, resists the temptation to romanticise rural poverty. Instead, she lets the elderly shepherds speak for themselves, and their candid, often hilarious testimony is the film's greatest strength.</p></blockquote><p>Find the words in the text that mean:</p><p>(a) very careful and detailed</p><p>(b) a gradual loss of strength, size or importance</p><p>(c) to present something as more attractive than it really is</p><p>(d) honest and direct</p>", rule: "[1] per correct item. True/false items: a correct choice AND a relevant brief quotation from the text are both needed for [1]; no mark for True/False alone. 'Find the word' items: the exact word from the text only - extra words that change the meaning score [0]. Short answers: accept OWTTE; answers lifted from the text are acceptable when they are precise.", pts: ["(a) meticulous [1]", "(b) decline [1]", "(c) romanticise [1]", "(d) candid [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "'After losing the final, the coach said the team would have to go back to the drawing board.' This means the team will:", options: ["buy new equipment", "start planning again from the beginning", "take art lessons", "give up competing"], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (start planning again from the beginning) - To go back to the drawing board means to start again with a new plan after a failure."] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Explain the meaning of the idiom in each sentence.<br>(a) The new manager really hit the ground running.<br>(b) Funding for the project is still up in the air.<br>(c) The rise in bus fares was the last straw for many commuters.", rule: "[1] per correct meaning in context. Accept OWTTE.", pts: ["(a) started work quickly and effectively from the very beginning [1]", "(b) still uncertain / undecided [1]", "(c) the final problem in a series that made people lose patience [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", t: "A student is writing a formal report. Replace each informal word or phrase in brackets with a more formal alternative: 'The survey showed that (a lot of) students (want) longer breaks, and that (kids) in the lower years (get) more homework than expected.'", rule: "[1] per suitable formal alternative that keeps the meaning.", pts: ["(a) many / a considerable number of [1]", "(b) would like / requested / favour [1]", "(c) pupils / younger students [1]", "(d) receive / are given [1]"] },
      { u: "b", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Choose the word that best completes the sentence: 'The government has ______ measures to reduce air pollution in city centres.'", options: ["done", "made", "taken", "put"], answer: 2, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: C (taken) - 'Take measures' is the standard collocation."] },
      { u: "b", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "A new café run by young people has opened in your neighbourhood. Write a text giving your opinion of it. Choose one text type: review for a local website, blog post, or article for the school magazine. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High A needs precise, varied vocabulary (evaluative adjectives, sensory description, some idiomatic expressions used naturally); high B needs a balanced evaluation with specific details and a recommendation; high C needs the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: describes atmosphere, menu, prices, service and the young people behind the project.", "Message: balanced evaluation with specific examples and a clear recommendation or rating.", "Vocabulary: precise adjectives (cosy, overpriced, impeccable) rather than 'nice' or 'good'; sensory description of food and drink.", "Idiom used naturally, e.g. 'a hidden gem', 'worth every penny'.", "Review conventions: title, name and location, star rating, evaluative language, recommendation.", "Blog conventions: title, personal anecdote, photos referred to, comments invited.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, lead paragraph, quotations from the owners."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Combine each pair of sentences into one sentence, using the word or phrase given.<br>(a) The festival was cancelled. The weather was terrible. (because of)<br>(b) She had never acted before. She won the lead role. (Although)<br>(c) The library reopened last month. It now has a café. (which)", rule: "[1] per correctly combined sentence with the meaning kept and correct punctuation.", pts: ["(a) The festival was cancelled because of the terrible weather [1]", "(b) Although she had never acted before, she won the lead role [1]", "(c) The library, which reopened last month, now has a café [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "<blockquote><p>Bike-sharing schemes have spread rapidly across Europe. They reduce traffic and, (a) ______, they encourage people to exercise. In the town of Valmont, (b) ______, car journeys in the centre fell by 8% in the first year. (c) ______, not every scheme has succeeded: some have suffered from vandalism and theft and, (d) ______, have been forced to close.</p><p><i>Valmont is fictional.</i></p></blockquote><p>Complete gaps (a)-(d) with the most appropriate word or phrase from the list. There are more words than gaps.</p><p>in addition &nbsp; for instance &nbsp; however &nbsp; as a result &nbsp; whose</p>", rule: "[1] per correct gap. Only words from the list are accepted.", pts: ["(a) in addition [1]", "(b) for instance [1]", "(c) However [1]", "(d) as a result [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Which sentence contains a non-defining relative clause?", options: ["The student who won the prize is my cousin.", "My cousin, who lives in Cairo, won the prize.", "The prize that she won was a laptop.", "Students who revise regularly usually do well."], answer: 1, rule: "[1] for the correct letter only.", pts: ["Answer: B (My cousin, who lives in Cairo, won the prize.) - A non-defining clause adds extra information about an already identified noun and is separated by commas."] },
      { u: "c", s: "A", m: "1+1+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "Rewrite each sentence for emphasis, beginning with the words given.<br>(a) I had hardly sat down when the phone rang. → Hardly ...<br>(b) We realised the danger only later. → Only later ...<br>(c) You should not open this door under any circumstances. → Under no circumstances ...", rule: "[1] per correct inversion with the meaning kept.", pts: ["(a) Hardly had I sat down when the phone rang [1]", "(b) Only later did we realise the danger [1]", "(c) Under no circumstances should you open this door [1]"] },
      { u: "c", s: "C", m: "12+12+6", paper: "P1", t: "Your school is holding a debate on the motion 'Teenagers should be allowed to vote at sixteen'. Write a text for or against the motion. Choose one text type: debate speech, opinion article, or letter to a national newspaper. Write 450-600 words.", rule: "Criterion A Language [12], Criterion B Message [12], Criterion C Conceptual understanding [6] = 30. High A needs a range of complex sentences (relative and participle clauses, conditionals, inversion for emphasis) used accurately; high B needs a logically organised argument with effective cohesive devices; high C needs the chosen type's conventions.", pts: ["Message: clear position on the motion with two or three developed arguments (maturity, taxes and responsibilities, civic education, influence of parents).", "Message: rebuttal of the opposing side and a strong conclusion.", "Cohesion: varied connectors for addition, contrast, consequence and concession (furthermore, nevertheless, consequently, admittedly).", "Language: complex sentences such as 'Not only do sixteen-year-olds pay taxes, but they also...' and conditionals.", "Debate speech conventions: addressing the chair and audience, stating the motion, signposting, rebuttal, closing appeal.", "Article conventions: headline, byline, provocative opening, subheadings.", "Letter conventions: 'Dear Editor', formal register, reference to the debate, formal close with name."] },
    ],
  },
});
